-- ID 518: Property Transactions: Basis — ADS vs GDS depreciation impact
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornton Industries places a $40,000 asset in service and elects the Alternative Depreciation System (ADS), while a competitor, Waverly Corp., places an identical $40,000 asset in service using the General Depreciation System (GDS) with 200% declining balance. Both assets have a 5-year GDS recovery period and a 9-year ADS recovery period. How does Thornton''s ADS election affect its depreciation deductions compared to Waverly''s GDS treatment, and what is the most likely reason a company would voluntarily choose the slower method?',
  choices = '["No meaningful difference — both methods produce the same total deduction, just with different labels on the tax return", "Thornton claims larger deductions in early years because ADS uses accelerated rates over a longer recovery period, benefiting cash flow", "Thornton claims smaller annual deductions spread over 9 years using straight-line, while Waverly front-loads deductions using 200% declining balance over 5 years — a company might elect ADS to avoid generating losses that trigger AMT adjustments or to preserve NOLs for more valuable future use", "Thornton deducts the full $40,000 immediately under ADS while Waverly must spread deductions over 5 years, because ADS is more favorable than GDS for all asset types"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): ADS requires straight-line depreciation over a longer recovery period (9 years for this asset class vs. 5 years under GDS), producing smaller annual deductions. Waverly''s GDS treatment uses 200% declining balance over 5 years, front-loading deductions into early years. While GDS is generally more favorable for reducing current tax, a company might voluntarily elect ADS to avoid creating net operating losses it cannot use, to reduce AMT preference items (the depreciation adjustment is eliminated under ADS), or to smooth income for financial reporting purposes. The total depreciation over the asset''s life is identical ($40,000), but the timing difference significantly affects cash flow and tax planning. Choice A is incorrect because while total deductions are equal, the timing difference has real economic value. Choice B reverses the relationship — ADS uses straight-line, which is slower, not faster. Choice D is incorrect because ADS does not allow immediate expensing; it is the slower of the two methods.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 518;

-- ID 599: Property Transactions: Basis — Personal-to-business conversion basis analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Caldwell converts a personal automobile (original cost $35,000, current FMV $22,000) to exclusive business use. Her accountant sets the depreciable basis at $22,000 rather than $35,000. Caldwell asks why the $13,000 decline in value cannot be recovered through depreciation. What is the analytical basis for using FMV instead of cost, and how would the result differ if the automobile''s FMV had been $38,000 at conversion?',
  choices = '["The $22,000 basis applies because the tax code always uses FMV for converted property; if FMV were $38,000, the basis would be $38,000 to reflect the higher market value", "The $22,000 basis applies because the $13,000 decline occurred during personal use, when losses are nondeductible — allowing cost basis would let Caldwell recover a personal loss through business deductions; if FMV were $38,000, the basis would be the original cost of $35,000 because the lower-of-cost-or-FMV rule prevents stepping up basis above cost", "The $35,000 cost is always the correct depreciable basis regardless of FMV, because the original purchase price establishes permanent basis for all purposes", "The basis is $0 because personal automobiles cannot be converted to business use under any circumstances"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): When personal property is converted to business use, the depreciable basis is the lower of the taxpayer''s adjusted basis (cost) or FMV at conversion. Caldwell''s cost ($35,000) exceeds FMV ($22,000), so FMV controls. The policy rationale is that the $13,000 decline is a personal loss — it occurred while the car served a nondeductible personal purpose. Allowing depreciation on the full $35,000 would effectively subsidize a personal loss through business deductions, which IRC Section 165 prohibits. If FMV were $38,000, the lower-of rule would select cost ($35,000) because the code does not allow taxpayers to increase basis above cost through appreciation during personal use — that unrealized gain has not been taxed. Choice A is incorrect because FMV is not always used; cost controls when cost is lower. Choice C is incorrect because the lower-of rule specifically overrides cost when FMV is lower at conversion. Choice D is incorrect because personal vehicles are routinely converted to business use.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 599;

-- ID 1286: Property Transactions: Basis — Bonus depreciation vs Section 179 income limitation
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Morrison, a sole proprietor, places $800,000 of qualifying equipment in service during 2025. Her business has a net loss of $50,000 before any depreciation deductions. Morrison''s tax advisor recommends 40% bonus depreciation instead of Section 179. What is the most likely effect of choosing bonus depreciation over Section 179 in this loss scenario, and what factor drives the advisor''s recommendation?',
  choices = '["Section 179 would be more advantageous because it has no income limitation and can create or increase a net operating loss", "Bonus depreciation can create or increase a net operating loss because it has no taxable income limitation, whereas Section 179 is limited to taxable income from active trades or businesses — in a loss year, Section 179 would produce $0 current deduction while bonus depreciation generates a deduction that deepens the NOL for carryforward", "Both produce identical results because the income limitation applies equally to bonus depreciation and Section 179", "Neither deduction is available in a loss year because all depreciation methods require positive taxable income"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The critical distinction is the taxable income limitation. Section 179 deductions cannot exceed the aggregate taxable income from the taxpayer''s active trades or businesses. With a $50,000 net loss, Morrison''s Section 179 deduction is limited to $0 (the excess carries forward). Bonus depreciation under Section 168(k) has no taxable income limitation — it applies regardless of profitability and can create or increase a net operating loss. For Morrison, 40% bonus depreciation on $800,000 produces a $320,000 deduction that deepens the NOL, which carries forward indefinitely (subject to the 80% limitation in future profitable years). This NOL-generating capability is the key analytical factor driving the advisor''s recommendation. Choice A reverses the income limitation rules — Section 179 has the limitation, not bonus depreciation. Choice C is incorrect because the income limitation applies only to Section 179. Choice D is incorrect because bonus depreciation has no income requirement.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1286;

