-- ID 543: Individual Taxation: Income — Social Security benefit taxability threshold analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Hargrove, a retired single taxpayer, receives $24,000 in Social Security benefits. In 2025, her provisional income (AGI + nontaxable interest + 50% of SS benefits) is $30,000. In 2026, she begins withdrawing from a traditional IRA, increasing her provisional income to $48,000. How does the increase in provisional income affect the percentage of Social Security benefits included in Hargrove''s gross income, and what causes the jump between inclusion tiers?',
  choices = '["No change — Social Security benefits are always fully excluded from gross income regardless of other income sources", "In 2025, 50% of benefits ($12,000) are includible because provisional income falls between $25,000 and $34,000; in 2026, up to 85% of benefits ($20,400) are includible because provisional income exceeds $34,000 — the IRA withdrawals pushed Hargrove past the second threshold, nearly doubling the taxable portion of her Social Security income", "In both years, 100% of benefits are includible because any provisional income above $25,000 triggers full inclusion", "In 2025, 85% is includible and in 2026, 50% is includible, because higher provisional income reduces the taxable percentage as a form of progressive relief"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Social Security taxability uses two provisional income thresholds for single filers: $25,000 and $34,000. In 2025, Hargrove''s provisional income of $30,000 falls between these thresholds, triggering the first tier — up to 50% of benefits ($12,000) are includible. In 2026, the IRA withdrawals push provisional income to $48,000, well above the $34,000 second threshold, triggering up to 85% inclusion ($20,400). The analytical insight is that IRA distributions create a cascading tax effect: they are fully taxable on their own and simultaneously increase the taxable portion of Social Security benefits. This interaction is a common retirement planning trap — the marginal tax rate on each additional dollar of IRA withdrawal effectively exceeds the statutory rate. Choice A is incorrect because benefits are partially taxable above the thresholds. Choice C is incorrect because inclusion is capped at 85%, not 100%. Choice D reverses the tiers — higher income increases the taxable percentage.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 543;

-- ID 1290: Individual Taxation: Income — Above-the-line deduction AGI impact analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Prescott, a single taxpayer with $95,000 in wages, purchases a qualifying U.S.-manufactured vehicle and pays $4,200 in auto loan interest during 2026. Prescott also pays $8,000 in state income taxes and $3,000 in mortgage interest. Under H.R. 1 as extended, the auto loan interest qualifies as an above-the-line deduction. How does the above-the-line classification of the auto loan interest affect Prescott''s AGI differently than the other deductible items, and why is this distinction significant?',
  choices = '["All four items reduce AGI equally because any deductible expense lowers adjusted gross income", "The $4,200 auto loan interest reduces AGI to $90,800 because above-the-line deductions are subtracted before AGI is calculated, while state taxes and mortgage interest are itemized deductions that reduce taxable income below the line — the lower AGI may expand eligibility for income-sensitive tax benefits that phase out based on AGI", "State income taxes reduce AGI while auto loan interest is an itemized deduction, because interest expenses are always below-the-line deductions", "The auto loan interest provides no benefit because above-the-line deductions are less valuable than itemized deductions in all circumstances"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Above-the-line deductions (adjustments to income) are subtracted from gross income to arrive at AGI, while itemized deductions are subtracted from AGI to arrive at taxable income. The $4,200 auto loan interest reduces Prescott''s AGI from $95,000 to $90,800. State income taxes ($8,000) and mortgage interest ($3,000) are itemized deductions — they reduce taxable income but do not affect AGI. The significance is that many tax benefits (Roth IRA eligibility, education credits, rental loss allowance, Social Security taxability thresholds) use AGI as the measuring stick. A lower AGI can preserve or increase these benefits, making above-the-line deductions more strategically valuable dollar-for-dollar than itemized deductions of the same amount. Choice A is incorrect because only above-the-line deductions reduce AGI. Choice C reverses the classifications. Choice D is incorrect because above-the-line deductions are generally more valuable, not less, due to their AGI impact.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1290;

-- ID 2615: Individual Taxation: Income — Section 61 gross income inclusion analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Delgado receives the following during 2025: $75,000 salary, $2,300 in municipal bond interest, $5,000 in gambling winnings, and a $10,000 gift from her grandmother. Her colleague argues that only the salary is gross income under Section 61. Analyze which items are includible in Delgado''s gross income and which are excluded, identifying the specific Code provision that governs each exclusion.',
  choices = '["All four items ($92,300) are gross income because Section 61 defines income as all receipts from whatever source derived with no exceptions", "Only the salary ($75,000) is gross income because wages are the only income specifically listed in Section 61", "The salary ($75,000) and gambling winnings ($5,000) are gross income totaling $80,000 — municipal bond interest is excluded under Section 103 and the gift is excluded under Section 102; Section 61''s broad definition captures all income unless a specific exclusion provision applies", "The salary ($75,000), gambling winnings ($5,000), and gift ($10,000) are gross income totaling $90,000 — only municipal bond interest is excluded because it is the only item with a statutory exclusion"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Section 61 casts the widest possible net — "all income from whatever source derived" — but the Code contains specific exclusion provisions that override this default. The salary and gambling winnings have no exclusion and are fully includible. Municipal bond interest is excluded under Section 103, which exempts interest on state and local obligations to reduce borrowing costs for governments. The gift is excluded under Section 102, which excludes the value of property acquired by gift, bequest, devise, or inheritance from gross income. The analytical framework is: start with Section 61''s broad inclusion, then check for specific exclusions. The colleague''s argument fails because it ignores gambling winnings (also gross income) and correctly excludes two items but for the wrong reason. Choice A is incorrect because Section 61 is subject to specific exclusion provisions throughout the Code. Choice B is incorrect because Section 61 explicitly lists many income types beyond wages, including gambling. Choice D incorrectly includes the gift — Section 102 specifically excludes gifts from gross income.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 2615;

