-- ID 489: Circular 230 — Error notification obligation analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Martinez, a CPA, discovers an error on a client''s previously filed return. In Scenario A, the error resulted in a $500 overpayment of tax (the client paid too much). In Scenario B, the error resulted in a $500 underpayment of tax (the client paid too little). Under Circular 230, how does Martinez''s obligation differ between these two scenarios, and what is the rationale for treating them the same or differently?',
  choices = '["In both scenarios, Martinez must file an amended return without the client''s consent because Circular 230 requires practitioners to correct all errors regardless of direction", "In Scenario A, Martinez has no obligation because the error favored the government, but in Scenario B, Martinez must report the error directly to the IRS within 30 days because underpayments require immediate disclosure", "In both scenarios, Martinez must promptly notify the client of the error and advise corrective action — Circular 230 does not distinguish between overpayments and underpayments because the practitioner''s duty runs to the client, not the IRS, and the decision to file an amended return ultimately belongs to the client", "In Scenario A, Martinez must notify the client, but in Scenario B, Martinez may remain silent because practitioners have no obligation regarding underpayment errors on previously filed returns"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under Circular 230 Section 10.21, a practitioner who discovers an error or omission on a client''s return must promptly notify the client of the error and advise the client of the consequences and corrective measures. This obligation applies regardless of whether the error resulted in an overpayment or underpayment. The key analytical point is that the duty runs to the client, not the IRS — the practitioner is not required to file an amended return or report directly to the IRS. The client retains the ultimate decision on whether to file an amended return. Treating both scenarios identically reflects the principle that the practitioner''s advisory role does not vary based on which party benefited from the mistake. Choice A is incorrect because the practitioner cannot file an amended return without client consent. Choice B is incorrect because there is no 30-day direct reporting requirement to the IRS. Choice D reverses the analysis and incorrectly suggests practitioners may ignore underpayment errors.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 489;

-- ID 491: Circular 230 — DIF score audit risk analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Vasquez, a CPA, is advising two clients about audit risk. Client Archer is a sole proprietor reporting $180,000 in revenue with $165,000 in deductions (91% expense ratio) and a home office deduction. Client Briggs is a W-2 employee earning $180,000 who takes the standard deduction. Both filed timely. Analyze how the IRS Discriminant Index Function (DIF) scoring system likely treats these two returns differently, and which factor most increases Archer''s audit risk.',
  choices = '["Both returns have identical audit risk because DIF scores are assigned randomly without regard to return characteristics", "Briggs faces higher audit risk because W-2 income is more closely scrutinized than self-employment income under DIF scoring", "Archer''s return likely generates a significantly higher DIF score because the system identifies statistical outliers — the 91% expense-to-revenue ratio combined with a home office deduction deviates from norms for the income class, while Briggs''s straightforward W-2 return with a standard deduction has few characteristics that trigger elevated scoring", "Neither return faces audit risk because returns under $200,000 are exempt from DIF scoring"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The IRS Discriminant Index Function assigns scores based on how significantly a return''s characteristics deviate from statistical norms for comparable returns. Archer''s return presents multiple audit indicators: a 91% expense ratio is unusually high for $180,000 in self-employment revenue, and home office deductions are historically associated with elevated audit risk. Together, these deviations from the norm for the income class likely produce a high DIF score. Briggs''s return — simple W-2 income with a standard deduction — closely matches the statistical profile for similar returns, producing a low DIF score. The analytical key is that DIF does not examine absolute dollar amounts in isolation but rather compares each return''s profile against expected patterns. Choice A is incorrect because DIF scoring is specifically designed to be non-random. Choice B reverses the risk — self-employment returns with high deduction ratios face greater scrutiny. Choice D is incorrect because there is no income floor for DIF scoring; all returns receive scores.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 491;

-- ID 493: Circular 230 — PTIN penalty cumulative exposure analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Nakamura, a tax return preparer, prepared 380 compensated returns during the 2025 filing season but failed to include her Preparer Tax Identification Number (PTIN) on any of them. The penalty for failure to include a PTIN is $50 per return, up to $27,000 per calendar year. Analyze Nakamura''s total penalty exposure and explain how the annual cap affects a high-volume preparer differently than a low-volume preparer who omits the PTIN on only 30 returns.',
  choices = '["Nakamura owes $19,000 (380 × $50) because the penalty is calculated per return with no annual cap — high-volume preparers face unlimited exposure", "Nakamura owes $19,000 (380 × $50, below the $27,000 annual cap) while the low-volume preparer owes $1,500 (30 × $50) — the cap would limit exposure only if per-return penalties exceeded $27,000 (more than 540 returns); both preparers pay the full per-return amount because neither exceeds the ceiling, but the cap protects the highest-volume preparers from disproportionate penalties", "Both preparers owe $27,000 because the cap is a flat penalty applied regardless of the number of violations", "Nakamura owes $0 because the PTIN requirement is only a recommendation with no enforceable penalty"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The Section 6695(c) penalty is $50 per return, subject to a $27,000 annual cap. Nakamura''s 380 returns generate $19,000 in penalties (380 × $50), which is below the $27,000 cap — so the cap does not reduce her liability. The low-volume preparer owes $1,500 (30 × $50). The cap becomes relevant only when per-return penalties exceed $27,000 (i.e., more than 540 returns). The analytical insight is that the cap functions as a maximum exposure ceiling that protects only the highest-volume preparers, while most preparers pay the full per-return amount. This structure balances deterrence against proportionality. Choice A is incorrect because a $27,000 annual cap does exist under Section 6695(c), contrary to the claim of unlimited exposure. Choice C is incorrect because the cap is a maximum ceiling, not a flat penalty — actual liability depends on volume. Choice D is incorrect because the PTIN requirement carries an enforceable statutory penalty.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 493;

