-- ID 88: Partnerships — Carryover basis and subsequent sale analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Partner Ashworth contributes land with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and FMV of $95,000 to a 50/50 partnership with Partner Beckett, who contributes $95,000 cash. Three years later, the partnership sells the land for $110,000. Analyze how the carryover basis rule affects the total gain recognized and its allocation between the partners, compared to what would result if the partnership had instead taken a FMV basis in the contributed land.',
  choices = '["Under carryover basis, the partnership recognizes $80,000 total gain ($110,000 − $30,000), with the $65,000 pre-contribution gain allocated to Ashworth under Section 704(c) and the $15,000 post-contribution gain split equally; if the partnership had taken FMV basis, total gain would be only $15,000 split equally — the $65,000 difference represents appreciation that would escape taxation entirely without the carryover rule", "Under carryover basis, the partnership recognizes $15,000 gain ($110,000 − $95,000) split equally, because carryover basis means the FMV carries over from the contributing partner", "Both carryover and FMV basis produce identical results because the total gain recognized over time is always the same regardless of the basis method used", "Under carryover basis, Beckett bears the entire $80,000 gain because the contributing partner is exempt from gain on contributed property"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The carryover basis rule under Section 723 gives the partnership a $30,000 basis in the land (Ashworth''s adjusted basis), preserving the $65,000 of pre-contribution appreciation. When the partnership sells for $110,000, total gain is $80,000 ($110,000 − $30,000). Section 704(c) allocates the $65,000 built-in gain to Ashworth (who held the appreciation before contributing) and splits the $15,000 post-contribution gain equally ($7,500 each). If the partnership had taken a FMV basis of $95,000, total gain would be only $15,000 ($110,000 − $95,000), and the $65,000 of pre-contribution appreciation would permanently escape taxation. The carryover basis rule exists precisely to prevent this outcome — it ensures that economically accrued gain is eventually recognized by the partner who benefited from it. Choice B confuses carryover basis with FMV basis. Choice C is incorrect because FMV basis would permanently exclude the built-in gain. Choice D reverses the allocation — Section 704(c) assigns built-in gain to the contributing partner, not the other partner.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 88;

-- ID 587: Partnerships — Section 704(c) anti-shifting analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Greenfield contributes land with a basis of $30,000 and FMV of $100,000 to a three-equal-partner partnership. The other two partners, Hayes and Irvine, each contribute $100,000 in cash. When the partnership later sells the land for $120,000, the total gain is $90,000. Analyze how Section 704(c) prevents the shifting of Greenfield''s pre-contribution gain to Hayes and Irvine, and what would happen to each partner''s tax burden if Section 704(c) did not exist.',
  choices = '["Section 704(c) allocates $30,000 to each partner equally because equal partners must share all gains equally regardless of contribution history", "Section 704(c) allocates the $70,000 built-in gain entirely to Greenfield plus one-third of the $20,000 post-contribution gain ($6,667), while Hayes and Irvine each receive one-third of the $20,000 ($6,667) — without Section 704(c), the entire $90,000 would be split equally ($30,000 each), forcing Hayes and Irvine to pay tax on $23,333 of gain that economically accrued before they joined the partnership", "Section 704(c) eliminates Greenfield''s gain entirely because contributing appreciated property qualifies for permanent exclusion", "Section 704(c) shifts all $90,000 to Hayes and Irvine because they contributed cash and received no basis benefit from the land appreciation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 704(c) requires that the $70,000 of built-in gain ($100,000 FMV − $30,000 basis at contribution) be allocated to Greenfield, the contributing partner. The $20,000 of post-contribution gain ($120,000 sale price − $100,000 FMV at contribution) is shared equally among all three partners ($6,667 each). Greenfield''s total allocation is $76,667 ($70,000 + $6,667). Without Section 704(c), the entire $90,000 gain would be split equally at $30,000 each — meaning Hayes and Irvine would each bear $23,333 more in gain than the economic reality warrants ($30,000 − $6,667). Section 704(c) is an anti-abuse provision that prevents contributing partners from shifting their pre-existing appreciation to other partners through the partnership vehicle. Choice A ignores Section 704(c)''s purpose entirely. Choice C is incorrect because Section 704(c) defers and allocates gain, it does not exclude it. Choice D reverses the allocation — the built-in gain goes to the contributor, not the cash partners.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 587;

-- ID 589: Partnerships — Section 751 hot asset recharacterization analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Partner Langston sells her 25% partnership interest for $200,000. Her outside basis is $140,000, producing a $60,000 total gain. The partnership''s hot assets (unrealized receivables and substantially appreciated inventory) allocable to Langston have a built-in ordinary income of $40,000. Analyze how Section 751 recharacterizes what would otherwise be entirely capital gain, and explain why Congress enacted this provision.',
  choices = '["Section 751 has no effect because all gain on the sale of a partnership interest is automatically capital gain under Section 741", "Section 751 recharacterizes $40,000 of the $60,000 gain as ordinary income (attributable to hot assets) and leaves only $20,000 as capital gain — without this provision, Langston could convert ordinary income items into capital gain simply by selling the partnership interest rather than waiting for the partnership to collect receivables or sell inventory, exploiting the rate differential between ordinary and capital gains rates", "Section 751 converts the entire $60,000 to ordinary income because any partnership holding hot assets loses capital gain treatment entirely", "Section 751 reduces the total gain to $20,000 by excluding the hot asset portion from the calculation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 741 generally treats gain on the sale of a partnership interest as capital gain, but Section 751 overrides this by recharacterizing the portion attributable to "hot assets" — unrealized receivables and substantially appreciated inventory — as ordinary income. Langston''s $40,000 of hot asset built-in ordinary income is carved out and taxed as ordinary income, leaving $20,000 ($60,000 − $40,000) as capital gain. Without Section 751, Langston could effectively convert ordinary income into capital gain by selling the interest rather than waiting for the partnership to realize the income through operations. Congress enacted Section 751 to prevent this character conversion — ensuring that items that would be ordinary income if realized directly by the partnership retain their ordinary character when the interest is sold. Choice A is incorrect because Section 751 creates a critical exception to Section 741''s general rule. Choice C is incorrect because only the hot asset portion is recharacterized, not the entire gain. Choice D is incorrect because Section 751 recharacterizes gain, it does not reduce total gain.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 589;

