-- PROPERTY TRANSACTIONS: GAINS/LOSSES (3 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 523: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses — Section 1245 recapture character analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Dawson sells equipment (Section 1245 property) for $120,000. The original cost was $150,000 and accumulated depreciation is $80,000, giving an adjusted basis of $70,000 and a $50,000 gain. Analyze how Section 1245 recapture changes the character of this gain compared to what would result under Section 1231 alone, and explain the policy rationale for recapturing depreciation as ordinary income.',
  choices = '["Under Section 1231 alone, the entire $50,000 would be long-term capital gain taxed at preferential rates; Section 1245 has no effect on equipment because it applies only to real property", "Under Section 1231 alone, the entire $50,000 would qualify as Section 1231 gain (potentially capital gain); Section 1245 recaptures the full $50,000 as ordinary income because the gain does not exceed the $80,000 of accumulated depreciation — this prevents taxpayers from claiming ordinary deductions for depreciation and then converting the recovery into capital gain upon sale", "Section 1245 converts only $30,000 to ordinary income (the amount by which sale price exceeds original cost) and leaves $20,000 as Section 1231 gain", "Both Section 1231 and Section 1245 treat the entire $50,000 as capital gain because gains on business property are always capital in nature"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Without Section 1245, the $50,000 gain on this business equipment would flow through Section 1231 and potentially qualify for long-term capital gain rates. Section 1245 overrides this by recapturing gain as ordinary income to the extent of all prior depreciation claimed. Since the $50,000 gain is less than the $80,000 of accumulated depreciation, the entire $50,000 is ordinary income. The policy rationale is symmetry: depreciation deductions reduced ordinary income at rates up to 37%, so the recovery of those deductions should also be ordinary income — otherwise, taxpayers would deduct at ordinary rates and recognize the recoupment at the lower capital gains rate, creating an arbitrage. Choice A is incorrect because Section 1245 applies specifically to personal property (equipment), not real property. Choice C incorrectly calculates the recapture amount — Section 1245 recaptures all depreciation, not just gain above original cost. Choice D is incorrect because Section 1245 explicitly converts what would be capital gain into ordinary income.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 523;

-- ID 525: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses — Collectible vs standard capital gain rate comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Whitfield, a single taxpayer in the 35% ordinary income bracket, sells two assets held for more than one year: a rare coin collection for a $40,000 gain and publicly traded stock for a $40,000 gain. Both are long-term capital gains. Analyze how the tax treatment of the collectible gain differs from the stock gain, and calculate the additional tax Whitfield pays due to the collectible rate differential.',
  choices = '["Both gains are taxed at the same 20% long-term capital gains rate because all long-term capital assets receive identical treatment", "The coin collection gain is taxed at a maximum rate of 28% while the stock gain is taxed at 20% (for Whitfield''s bracket) — the $40,000 collectible gain produces $11,200 in tax versus $8,000 for the stock gain, costing Whitfield an additional $3,200; Congress set the higher rate for collectibles because they produce no productive economic activity compared to equity investments", "The coin collection gain is fully exempt from tax because collectibles held over one year qualify for a complete exclusion", "The stock gain is taxed at 28% while the collectible gain is taxed at 15%, because the Code penalizes financial asset gains more heavily than tangible asset gains"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Collectibles (art, coins, stamps, gems, antiques) held more than one year are subject to a maximum 28% capital gains rate under Section 1(h), compared to the 15%/20% rate that applies to most other long-term capital gains. For Whitfield, in the 35% ordinary bracket, the stock gain is taxed at 20% ($8,000) while the collectible gain is taxed at 28% ($11,200) — a $3,200 differential on identical gain amounts. The policy rationale is that Congress views collectibles as consumption-oriented assets rather than productive investment, warranting less favorable capital gains treatment. The 28% rate is still below ordinary rates, providing some benefit for long-term holding, but the reduced preference discourages tax-motivated investment in non-productive assets. Choice A is incorrect because the Code explicitly creates a separate, higher rate for collectibles. Choice C is incorrect because no complete exclusion exists for collectibles. Choice D reverses the rate relationship.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 525;

-- ID 527: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses — Section 1250 non-application to losses analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Patterson sells a commercial building (Section 1250 property) for $280,000. The original cost was $400,000 with accumulated depreciation of $100,000, giving an adjusted basis of $300,000 and a $20,000 loss. Patterson''s advisor explains that Section 1250 recapture does not apply. Analyze why Section 1250 recapture is irrelevant when property is sold at a loss, and how the loss is treated under the Section 1231 netting process.',
  choices = '["Section 1250 recapture applies to losses just as it applies to gains — the $20,000 loss is recharacterized as an ordinary loss under Section 1250", "Section 1250 recapture does not apply because there is no gain to recapture — recapture provisions only convert gain (up to the amount of prior depreciation) from capital to ordinary character, and a loss means the property was sold for less than its depreciated basis; the $20,000 loss enters the Section 1231 netting process as a Section 1231 loss, which is treated as ordinary loss if net Section 1231 losses exceed gains for the year", "The $20,000 loss is a capital loss subject to the $3,000 annual deduction limit because all real property losses are capital losses", "The loss is nondeductible because Section 1250 property that has been depreciated cannot generate a deductible loss"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 1250 recapture is a gain recharacterization provision — it converts what would otherwise be capital gain into ordinary income to the extent of "excess depreciation" (depreciation claimed above straight-line). When property is sold at a loss, there is no gain to recharacterize, so the recapture provision is simply inapplicable. The $20,000 loss ($280,000 sale price − $300,000 adjusted basis) enters the Section 1231 hotchpot. If Patterson''s net Section 1231 results show losses exceeding gains for the year, the net loss is treated as ordinary — deductible against wages and other ordinary income without the $3,000 capital loss limitation. If Section 1231 gains exceed losses, the net gain is capital. This favorable asymmetry (ordinary on net loss, capital on net gain) is the key benefit of Section 1231. Choice A is incorrect because recapture provisions apply only to gains. Choice C is incorrect because Section 1231 losses are ordinary, not capital. Choice D is incorrect because depreciated business property absolutely can generate deductible losses.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 527;

