-- ID 1260: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits — Filing status transition analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Tom''s wife died in March 2025. Tom has not remarried and maintains a home for his 8-year-old dependent son throughout 2025, 2026, and 2027. Analyze how Tom''s filing status changes across these three tax years and identify what event or condition triggers each transition.',
  choices = '["Tom files as Single in all three years because his spouse is deceased and he has not remarried", "Tom files as Married Filing Jointly in 2025, Qualifying Surviving Spouse in 2026, and Qualifying Surviving Spouse in 2027 — the QSS status is available indefinitely as long as Tom maintains a dependent child", "Tom files as Married Filing Jointly in 2025 (the year of death), Qualifying Surviving Spouse in 2026 (first year after death with a dependent child at home), and Head of Household in 2027 — QSS expires after the second year following the year of death, and Tom transitions to HOH because he still maintains a home for a dependent", "Tom files as Head of Household in all three years because maintaining a home for a dependent child always qualifies for HOH regardless of marital history"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The filing status transition follows three distinct phases. In 2025 (the year of death), Tom may file a joint return with his deceased spouse — Married Filing Jointly. In 2026, Tom qualifies as a Qualifying Surviving Spouse because it is within the two-year window after the year of death and he maintains a home for a dependent child. By 2027, the third year after death, the QSS status has expired (it is available only for the two tax years following the year of death). Tom then transitions to Head of Household because he is unmarried and maintains a household for a qualifying dependent. The trigger for each transition is the passage of time relative to the year of death, combined with the continuing requirement of maintaining a home for a dependent. Choice A is incorrect because Tom qualifies for more favorable statuses in 2025 and 2026. Choice B is incorrect because QSS is limited to two years following the year of death, not indefinite. Choice D is incorrect because MFJ and QSS are more favorable than HOH and are available in the earlier years.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 1260;

-- ID 1263: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits — Qualifying child vs qualifying relative test comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Karen, age 20 and a full-time college student, earned $18,000 from a summer job. She lives with her parents for 9 months of the year and does not provide more than half of her own support. Her parents'' AGI is $120,000. Karen''s parents claim her as a qualifying child. Karen''s neighbor, Alex, age 20 and NOT a student, also earned $18,000 and lives with his parents under identical circumstances. Analyze why Karen qualifies as a dependent but Alex likely does not, and identify which specific test creates the different outcome.',
  choices = '["Both qualify as dependents because the gross income test does not apply to any person under age 24", "Neither qualifies because earning $18,000 automatically disqualifies anyone from dependent status regardless of the test applied", "Karen qualifies as a qualifying child because there is no gross income test for qualifying children and she meets the age test as a full-time student under 24; Alex fails the qualifying child age test (not a student and over 19) and also fails the qualifying relative gross income test ($18,000 exceeds the $5,200 threshold) — the student status exception to the age test is the factor that creates the different outcome", "Karen qualifies as a qualifying relative while Alex qualifies as a qualifying child, because the student exception reverses which test applies to each person"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The qualifying child test has four requirements: relationship, age, residency, and support — but critically, no gross income test. Karen meets the age test because she is a full-time student under age 24 (the student exception extends the age limit from 19 to 24). Her $18,000 of income is irrelevant under the qualifying child rules. Alex, at age 20 but not a student, fails the qualifying child age test (which normally requires under 19 for non-students). Alex must then be tested under the qualifying relative rules, which impose a gross income test — his $18,000 exceeds the threshold ($5,200 for 2025), disqualifying him. The analytical insight is that Karen''s full-time student status activates the age exception, bypassing the gross income limitation entirely. Without student status, Alex has no path to dependency. Choice A is incorrect because the gross income test does not apply to qualifying children specifically, but it does apply to qualifying relatives. Choice B is incorrect because income alone does not disqualify a qualifying child. Choice D reverses the applicable tests.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 1263;

-- ID 1265: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits — HOH parent residency exception analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'David, who is unmarried, provides 100% of the financial support for his elderly mother who lives in a separate nursing home. David''s friend, Ellen, also unmarried, provides 100% of support for her elderly aunt who also lives in a separate nursing home. Both claim Head of Household status. Analyze why the parent exception to the residency requirement allows David to claim HOH but prevents Ellen from doing so, and what policy rationale justifies treating parents differently from other qualifying relatives.',
  choices = '["Both David and Ellen qualify for HOH because supporting any relative in a nursing home satisfies the residency requirement", "Neither qualifies for HOH because the dependent must physically live in the taxpayer''s home for all dependents, including parents", "David qualifies because a dependent parent does NOT need to live with the taxpayer for HOH purposes — the Code carves out this exception recognizing that aging parents often require care facilities; Ellen does not qualify because her aunt is not a parent, and the general rule requires the dependent to live in the taxpayer''s home for more than half the year, with no exception for aunts or other qualifying relatives", "Ellen qualifies but David does not, because the parent exception actually requires the parent to live with the taxpayer while other relatives may live separately"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The Head of Household rules generally require the qualifying person to live in the taxpayer''s home for more than half the year. However, Section 2(b)(1)(B) creates a specific exception for dependent parents — a parent does not need to live with the taxpayer, so long as the taxpayer maintains the household in which the parent lives (such as paying nursing home costs). This exception recognizes the practical reality that elderly parents often require specialized care that cannot be provided in the taxpayer''s home. No similar exception exists for aunts, cousins, or other qualifying relatives — Ellen''s aunt must physically reside in Ellen''s home for more than half the year to satisfy the HOH residency requirement. The policy rationale is that the parent-child obligation is uniquely strong in the Code, and Congress did not want to penalize taxpayers who fulfill this obligation through a care facility rather than in-home care. Choice A is incorrect because the nursing home exception applies only to parents. Choice B is incorrect because the parent exception explicitly overrides the general residency rule. Choice D reverses the rule entirely.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 1265;

