-- ID 573: C Corporations — UBIT three-part test comparative analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A 501(c)(3) hospital operates two side activities: (1) a parking garage open to the general public, generating $300,000 annually, and (2) a cafeteria serving only hospital patients, staff, and visitors, generating $200,000 annually. Analyze how the three-part Unrelated Business Income Tax test (trade or business, regularly carried on, not substantially related) applies to each activity differently, and identify which prong creates the distinction.',
  choices = '["Both activities are subject to UBIT because any revenue-generating activity by a tax-exempt organization is unrelated business income", "Neither activity is subject to UBIT because hospitals are fully exempt from all income taxation including UBIT", "The parking garage income is likely UBIT because it is a trade or business, regularly carried on, and serving the general public is not substantially related to the hospital''s exempt purpose of providing healthcare; the cafeteria income is likely NOT UBIT because while it meets the first two prongs, serving patients and staff is substantially related to the hospital''s healthcare mission — the ''substantially related'' prong is the key differentiator", "The cafeteria income is UBIT while the parking garage income is exempt, because food service is never related to healthcare while parking supports patient access"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The UBIT analysis applies a three-part test under Sections 512-514: (1) the activity must constitute a trade or business, (2) it must be regularly carried on, and (3) it must not be substantially related to the organization''s exempt purpose. Both the parking garage and cafeteria meet prongs one and two — they are regular commercial operations. The critical distinction lies in prong three. The cafeteria serving patients, staff, and visitors is substantially related to the hospital''s healthcare mission — patient nutrition is integral to medical care, and staff dining supports hospital operations. The parking garage serving the general public goes beyond the exempt purpose; public motorists have no connection to healthcare delivery. The ''substantially related'' analysis examines whether the activity contributes importantly to the exempt purpose, not merely whether it generates revenue for the organization. Choice A is incorrect because the three-part test must be satisfied. Choice B is incorrect because hospitals are subject to UBIT on unrelated activities. Choice D reverses the analysis of both activities.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 573;

-- ID 600: C Corporations — UBIT statutory exclusion analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A Section 501(c)(3) organization operates a thrift store staffed entirely by volunteers, selling only donated merchandise. The store generates $180,000 in annual net income. The organization''s board asks whether this income is subject to UBIT. Analyze which specific statutory exclusions shield the thrift store income from UBIT, and explain what would change if the organization hired paid staff to replace half of the volunteers.',
  choices = '["The income is fully subject to UBIT because any retail operation by a tax-exempt organization is an unrelated business regardless of staffing", "The thrift store qualifies for two separate statutory exclusions: the volunteer labor exclusion under Section 513(a)(1), which exempts businesses where substantially all work is performed by unpaid volunteers, and the donated merchandise exclusion under Section 513(a)(3), which exempts businesses that sell donated goods — either exclusion independently shields the income; if paid staff replaced half the volunteers, the volunteer exclusion would likely fail, but the donated merchandise exclusion would still protect the income as long as only donated goods are sold", "The income is exempt solely because thrift stores are specifically listed as a related activity for all 501(c)(3) organizations in the Code", "The income is always taxable regardless of volunteer labor or donated merchandise because retail sales cannot qualify for any UBIT exclusion"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Two independent statutory exclusions protect the thrift store income. First, Section 513(a)(1) excludes any trade or business where substantially all the work is performed without compensation (the volunteer exclusion). Second, Section 513(a)(3) excludes any trade or business that consists of selling donated merchandise (the donated goods exclusion). Either exclusion alone would shield the income. If paid staff replaced half the volunteers, the "substantially all" requirement of the volunteer exclusion would likely fail — but the donated merchandise exclusion would remain intact as long as the store sells only donated goods. This layered protection illustrates Congress''s intent to encourage charitable thrift operations through multiple safe harbors. Choice A is incorrect because the Code provides specific exclusions that override the general UBIT rules. Choice C is incorrect because there is no blanket thrift store exemption — the exclusions are based on operational characteristics (volunteer labor, donated goods), not the type of store. Choice D is incorrect because both exclusions are well-established in the statute.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 600;

-- ID 2781: C Corporations — Flat corporate rate vs individual rate comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Beta Corp, a C corporation, has taxable income of $500,000 for the current year. Its sole shareholder, Raines, is in the 37% individual tax bracket. Raines is deciding whether to operate as a C corporation or convert to an S corporation (pass-through). Analyze how the flat 21% corporate rate compares to individual rates for the same $500,000 of income, and identify the factor that makes the comparison more complex than simply comparing 21% to 37%.',
  choices = '["The C corporation always produces less total tax because 21% is lower than 37%, making C corporation status universally superior to pass-through treatment", "The C corporation pays $105,000 at 21%, but the comparison to pass-through taxation is incomplete because C corporation income faces double taxation — corporate tax plus shareholder tax on dividends (currently 20% plus 3.8% NIIT); the effective combined rate on distributed earnings can reach approximately 39.8%, exceeding the 37% individual rate; however, if earnings are retained rather than distributed, the single 21% rate provides a deferral advantage", "The S corporation always produces less total tax because pass-through entities pay no tax at the entity level and the 20% Section 199A deduction reduces the effective rate below 21%", "Both structures produce identical total tax because the Code is designed to ensure tax neutrality between entity types"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The flat 21% corporate rate produces $105,000 in corporate tax, seemingly favorable compared to 37% individual rates. However, this comparison ignores double taxation — when Beta Corp distributes earnings as dividends, Raines pays tax at qualified dividend rates (20%) plus the 3.8% Net Investment Income Tax, producing a combined effective rate of approximately 39.8% on distributed earnings (1 − (1−0.21)(1−0.238) ≈ 39.8%). This exceeds the 37% individual rate on pass-through income. The critical variable is distribution timing: if earnings are retained in the corporation, the shareholder-level tax is deferred, and the 21% rate provides a cash flow advantage. This deferral benefit diminishes as the expected retention period shortens. Choice A is incorrect because it ignores the second layer of taxation on distributions. Choice C oversimplifies — Section 199A has income limitations, phase-outs, and specified service business restrictions that may reduce or eliminate the deduction. Choice D is incorrect because entity choice creates materially different tax outcomes.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 2781;

