-- ID 556: Individual Taxation: Credits — 110% safe harbor threshold analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Fletcher, a self-employed consultant, had prior-year tax liability of $180,000 and expects current-year liability of $220,000. Fletcher plans to use the safe harbor for estimated tax payments to avoid penalties. Analyze why the 110% safe harbor applies to Fletcher instead of the 100% safe harbor, what income threshold triggers the higher percentage, and what the practical consequence is for Fletcher''s quarterly payment amounts.',
  choices = '["The 100% safe harbor applies to all taxpayers regardless of prior-year income — Fletcher must pay at least $180,000 in quarterly estimated taxes", "Fletcher must use the 110% safe harbor because her prior-year AGI exceeds $150,000 — this requires quarterly payments based on at least $198,000 (110% × $180,000), or $49,500 per quarter; Congress imposed the higher threshold to prevent high-income taxpayers from significantly underpaying current-year taxes by relying on a lower prior-year baseline, particularly when income is rising", "The 110% rule applies only to taxpayers with income under $150,000 to provide extra protection for lower-income filers", "Fletcher has no safe harbor available because self-employed taxpayers are excluded from the estimated tax safe harbor provisions"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The estimated tax safe harbor under Section 6654(d) allows taxpayers to avoid the underpayment penalty by paying at least 100% of prior-year tax liability in quarterly installments. However, when prior-year AGI exceeds $150,000 ($75,000 for MFS), the threshold increases to 110%. Fletcher''s $180,000 prior-year liability indicates AGI above $150,000, requiring payments of at least $198,000 annually ($180,000 × 110% = $198,000, or $49,500 per quarter). The policy rationale is that high-income taxpayers with rising incomes could otherwise pay minimal estimated taxes based on a much lower prior year, resulting in significant underpayment. The 110% rule narrows this gap. Fletcher could alternatively pay 90% of current-year liability ($198,000), but the prior-year method provides certainty. Choice A is incorrect because the 100% safe harbor does not apply when AGI exceeds $150,000. Choice C reverses the income threshold — 110% applies to higher income, not lower. Choice D is incorrect because self-employed taxpayers are fully eligible for the safe harbor.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 556;

-- ID 558: Individual Taxation: Credits — LLC vs AOTC eligibility comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Nguyen, a single taxpayer with AGI of $65,000, is enrolled part-time in a graduate certificate program and paid $8,500 in qualified tuition expenses during 2025. Nguyen''s advisor considers both the American Opportunity Tax Credit (AOTC) and the Lifetime Learning Credit (LLC). Analyze why the LLC is available to Nguyen but the AOTC is not, and compare the maximum credit amounts each would provide if both were available.',
  choices = '["Both credits are available and Nguyen should claim whichever produces the higher amount — the AOTC provides up to $2,500 while the LLC provides up to $2,000", "Neither credit is available because Nguyen''s AGI of $65,000 exceeds the phase-out threshold for both education credits", "The AOTC is available but the LLC is not, because the AOTC covers graduate programs while the LLC is limited to undergraduate education", "The LLC is available (providing up to $2,000, calculated as 20% of the first $10,000 in qualified expenses) because it has no restriction on graduate programs, enrollment intensity, or number of years claimed; the AOTC is unavailable because it requires enrollment at least half-time in a degree program during the first four years of postsecondary education — Nguyen''s graduate-level status and certificate (non-degree) program likely disqualify her on both counts"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): The AOTC under Section 25A(b) is restricted to the first four years of postsecondary education, requires at least half-time enrollment, and applies only to students pursuing a degree. Nguyen''s graduate certificate program fails multiple AOTC requirements: she is beyond the first four years, the certificate may not qualify as a degree program, and part-time enrollment may fall below the half-time threshold. The LLC under Section 25A(c) has none of these restrictions — it covers any postsecondary coursework (graduate, undergraduate, professional development), has no enrollment intensity requirement, and can be claimed for unlimited years. Nguyen''s $8,500 in qualified expenses generates a $1,700 LLC (20% × $8,500, up to the $2,000 maximum on $10,000). The LLC income phase-out begins above $80,000 for single filers, so Nguyen''s $65,000 AGI is below the phase-out. Choice A is incorrect because the AOTC has eligibility restrictions Nguyen cannot meet. Choice B is incorrect because $65,000 is below the phase-out threshold for both credits. Choice C reverses the credit restrictions entirely.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 558;

-- ID 560: Individual Taxation: Credits — Annualized income installment method analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Park, a self-employed consultant, earned 80% of her 2025 income in the fourth quarter due to a large project that closed in November. Her total tax liability for 2025 is $120,000. Under the standard equal-installment method, Park would owe $30,000 per quarter. Analyze how the annualized income installment method reduces Park''s penalty exposure for the first three quarters, and explain why this method exists.',
  choices = '["The annualized income installment method has no effect because all self-employed taxpayers must pay equal quarterly installments regardless of when income is earned", "The annualized method allows Park to base each quarterly payment on income actually earned through that quarter''s cutoff, annualized to a full-year equivalent — since only 20% of income was earned through the first three quarters, her required payments for Q1-Q3 are significantly lower than $30,000 each; the method exists because the standard equal-installment method assumes uniform income throughout the year, which unfairly penalizes taxpayers with seasonal or back-loaded income by requiring large payments before income is earned", "The annualized method eliminates all estimated tax requirements for taxpayers who earn more than 50% of income in the fourth quarter", "The annualized method doubles the required Q4 payment but eliminates Q1-Q3 payments entirely for seasonal businesses"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The annualized income installment method under Section 6654(d)(2) computes each quarter''s required payment based on income actually earned through that period, annualized to a full-year equivalent. For Park, who earned roughly 20% of income in Q1-Q3, her annualized income through September suggests a much lower annual liability than $120,000. This means her required installments for Q1-Q3 are proportionally lower — perhaps $6,000-$8,000 per quarter rather than $30,000. Without this method, Park would face underpayment penalties on the $22,000-$24,000 shortfall each quarter, despite having no ability to pay from income not yet earned. The method was enacted because the standard method''s assumption of uniform income throughout the year disproportionately burdens seasonal businesses, commissioned salespeople, and consultants with lumpy income patterns. The Q4 payment increases correspondingly to cover the full annual liability. Choice A is incorrect because the annualized method is a specific statutory alternative. Choice C is incorrect because the method adjusts payment timing, it does not eliminate the obligation. Choice D is incorrect because the method recalculates each quarter individually rather than eliminating three quarters entirely.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 560;

