-- ID 578: S Corporations — Distribution reclassification employment tax analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Crawford, the sole shareholder-employee of an S corporation, takes no salary and instead receives $200,000 in distributions. The IRS proposes to reclassify $120,000 of the distributions as reasonable compensation. Analyze the employment tax consequences of this reclassification for both Crawford and the S corporation, and explain why the IRS targets this arrangement.',
  choices = '["Reclassification has no tax effect because S corporation distributions and salary are taxed identically", "Reclassification would subject the $120,000 to Social Security tax (6.2% each for employer and employee, up to the wage base) and Medicare tax (1.45% each, plus 0.9% additional Medicare on amounts above $200,000) — the combined employment tax exposure on $120,000 is approximately $18,360 (employee share) plus $9,180 (employer share); the IRS targets this arrangement because shareholder-employees use zero-salary/high-distribution strategies to avoid FICA taxes that fund Social Security and Medicare, which pass-through distributions do not trigger", "Reclassification converts the $120,000 from ordinary income to capital gains, reducing Crawford''s overall tax rate", "The IRS cannot reclassify S corporation distributions because shareholders have absolute discretion over the salary/distribution split"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): S corporation distributions are not subject to employment taxes (FICA), while salary is subject to both the employer and employee shares of Social Security (6.2% each, up to the wage base of $168,600 in 2024) and Medicare (1.45% each, plus 0.9% additional Medicare tax on employee wages above $200,000). When Crawford takes $200,000 entirely as distributions with zero salary, no employment taxes are paid. IRS reclassification of $120,000 as compensation triggers approximately $18,360 in employee-side FICA ($120,000 × 15.3%) plus an employer-side match of approximately $9,180 ($120,000 × 7.65%). The IRS targets this through the "reasonable compensation" doctrine because zero-salary arrangements are economically implausible — Crawford is performing services that generate the $200,000, so a reasonable salary must be paid before distributions. This is one of the most litigated areas in S corporation tax law. Choice A is incorrect because the employment tax difference is the entire motivation for the strategy. Choice C is incorrect because reclassification creates ordinary compensation income, not capital gains. Choice D is incorrect because courts consistently uphold IRS authority to reclassify distributions as compensation when no reasonable salary is paid.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 578;

-- ID 580: S Corporations — AAA and AE&P distribution ordering analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Mercer is the sole shareholder of an S corporation with accumulated adjustments account (AAA) of $50,000 and accumulated earnings and profits (AE&P) of $30,000 from prior C corporation years. Stock basis is $80,000. The corporation distributes $90,000 to Mercer. Analyze the ordering rules that determine the tax treatment of this distribution, and explain what would change if the distribution were $95,000 instead of $90,000.',
  choices = '["The entire $90,000 is a taxable dividend because any S corporation with AE&P treats all distributions as dividends", "The entire $90,000 is tax-free return of capital because S corporation distributions are never taxable to shareholders", "At $90,000: the first $50,000 comes from AAA (tax-free return of basis), the next $30,000 comes from AE&P (taxable as a dividend), and the remaining $10,000 reduces stock basis (tax-free return of capital); at $95,000: the same ordering applies through AAA and AE&P ($80,000), then $10,000 reduces remaining basis to $0, and the final $5,000 exceeds basis and is taxed as capital gain — demonstrating that the ordering rules create three distinct layers of tax treatment", "The AE&P is distributed first ($30,000 as dividend), then AAA ($50,000 tax-free), then the remaining $10,000 as capital gain"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The S corporation distribution ordering rules create a layered tax analysis. First, distributions come from AAA — the cumulative post-S-election income that has already been taxed to the shareholder. The $50,000 AAA distribution is tax-free (reduces basis from $80,000 to $30,000). Second, distributions come from AE&P — earnings accumulated during prior C corporation years that were never distributed. The $30,000 AE&P distribution is a taxable dividend (basis remains $30,000). Third, any remaining distribution reduces stock basis — the $10,000 excess reduces basis from $30,000 to $20,000, tax-free as return of capital. At $95,000, the same layers apply, but the final $5,000 exceeds remaining basis ($80,000 − $50,000 AAA − $10,000 remaining = $20,000 basis after AAA; minus $30,000 AE&P leaves $20,000; $15,000 return of capital exhausts basis; $5,000 excess = capital gain). The ordering matters because it determines what is tax-free, what is dividend, and what is capital gain. Choice A is incorrect because AAA distributions are tax-free. Choice B is incorrect because AE&P distributions are dividends. Choice D reverses the ordering — AAA is distributed before AE&P.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 580;

-- ID 584: S Corporations — Tax-exempt income AAA vs OAA flow-through analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Greenfield Solar Corp., an S corporation, earns $150,000 of ordinary business income and $8,500 of tax-exempt interest from municipal bonds during 2025. Palmer, the sole shareholder, asks why the tax-exempt interest appears on Schedule K-1 if it is not taxable. Analyze how tax-exempt income flows through differently than ordinary income in terms of the Accumulated Adjustments Account (AAA) versus the Other Adjustments Account (OAA), and explain the impact on Palmer''s stock basis.',
  choices = '["Both items increase AAA equally because all income items flow through the same account regardless of tax character", "The $150,000 ordinary income increases AAA, while the $8,500 tax-exempt interest increases the Other Adjustments Account (OAA) rather than AAA — both increase Palmer''s stock basis by a combined $158,500, but the separation matters because AAA tracks previously taxed income available for tax-free distribution, while OAA tracks tax-exempt income that has never been taxed; distributions from OAA are also tax-free but for a different reason — they represent income that was exempt at the entity level and should not be taxed upon distribution either", "Tax-exempt income does not affect any S corporation accounts or shareholder basis because it is excluded from all computations", "Tax-exempt interest reduces AAA because the S corporation did not pay tax on it, creating a negative adjustment"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The S corporation maintains separate accounts to track the tax character of income flowing through to shareholders. Ordinary business income ($150,000) increases AAA — this is income that has been taxed at the shareholder level and can later be distributed tax-free as a return of previously taxed income. Tax-exempt interest ($8,500) bypasses AAA entirely and increases OAA — a separate account for items that were never subject to tax. Both increase Palmer''s stock basis, which is critical for determining the tax treatment of future distributions and the deductibility of losses. The K-1 reports tax-exempt interest because basis tracking requires all economic items. The separation between AAA and OAA prevents a distortion: if tax-exempt income were mixed into AAA, it could be distributed and treated as previously taxed income when it was actually never-taxed income — the economic result is the same (tax-free distribution), but the accounting distinction prevents confusion in ordering rules when AE&P exists. Choice A is incorrect because AAA and OAA serve different tracking purposes. Choice C is incorrect because tax-exempt income absolutely affects basis and OAA. Choice D is incorrect because tax-exempt income is a positive OAA adjustment, not a negative AAA adjustment.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 584;

