-- LEGAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (3 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 1169: Legal Duties and Responsibilities — Disclosed vs undisclosed position standard comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax preparer takes a position on a client''s return. In Scenario A, the position is disclosed on Form 8275 or 8275-R. In Scenario B, the identical position is not disclosed. Analyze how the minimum standard for avoiding the Section 6694(a) preparer penalty differs between disclosed and undisclosed positions, and explain why the Code imposes a stricter standard on undisclosed positions.',
  choices = '["Both scenarios require the same ''more likely than not'' standard because disclosure has no effect on the applicable penalty threshold", "The undisclosed position must meet a ''substantial authority'' standard (roughly 40% likelihood of being sustained), while the disclosed position need only meet a ''reasonable basis'' standard (roughly 20% likelihood) — the difference exists because disclosure gives the IRS notice of the aggressive position, allowing it to evaluate the issue on audit; without disclosure, the position may escape scrutiny entirely, so the Code demands greater confidence that it would withstand challenge", "The disclosed position requires ''substantial authority'' while the undisclosed position requires only ''reasonable basis,'' because hidden positions receive more lenient treatment to encourage privacy", "Both scenarios require only a ''realistic possibility of success'' standard (33%) because Section 6694 uses a single uniform threshold"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 6694(a) establishes a two-tier standard based on disclosure. Undisclosed positions must meet the "substantial authority" standard — approximately a 40% chance of being sustained on the merits. Disclosed positions (via Form 8275 or 8275-R) need only meet the lower "reasonable basis" standard — approximately a 20% chance. The policy rationale is transparency-based: when a position is disclosed, the IRS has notice and can allocate examination resources to evaluate it. The preparer has demonstrated good faith by flagging the issue rather than burying it. Without disclosure, the position may never be examined, allowing positions with weak authority to go unchallenged. Requiring higher confidence for undisclosed positions compensates for this reduced oversight. The penalty for violating Section 6694(a) is the greater of $1,000 or 50% of the preparer''s fee. Choice A is incorrect because the standard explicitly varies with disclosure. Choice C reverses the standards — disclosure merits a lower threshold, not higher. Choice D is incorrect because the "realistic possibility" standard was replaced by the current framework.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1169;

-- ID 1171: Legal Duties and Responsibilities — Per-credit due diligence penalty analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A preparer fails to satisfy due diligence requirements under Section 6695(g) for three credits claimed on the same return: the Earned Income Credit, the Child Tax Credit, and the American Opportunity Credit. The penalty is $560 per failure for the current year. Analyze why the penalty is assessed per credit rather than per return, and calculate the preparer''s total exposure if this pattern repeats across 40 returns during the filing season.',
  choices = '["The penalty is $560 per return regardless of how many credits are claimed, because due diligence is a return-level obligation — total exposure for 40 returns is $22,400", "The penalty is assessed per credit per return because each credit has its own due diligence requirements, separate documentation standards, and distinct eligibility criteria — failing three credits on one return produces $1,680 (3 × $560); across 40 returns with the same failures, total exposure is $67,200 (40 × $1,680); the per-credit structure exists because Congress recognized that each credit presents independent fraud risk and requires the preparer to verify different eligibility factors (income, dependents, enrollment) — a per-return penalty would create insufficient deterrence for preparers who routinely rubber-stamp multiple credits without verification", "The penalty applies only once per preparer per tax season, capped at $560 total regardless of the number of returns or credits", "The penalty is assessed per credit but only for the Earned Income Credit — the CTC and AOTC are exempt from due diligence penalties"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 6695(g) imposes the due diligence penalty separately for each applicable credit on each return. The four covered credits (EIC, CTC/ACTC/ODC, AOTC, and HOH filing status) each require independent verification of different eligibility factors. For EIC, the preparer must verify income, filing status, and qualifying children. For CTC, the preparer must verify dependent eligibility and income thresholds. For AOTC, the preparer must verify enrollment status, educational institution, and qualified expenses. A preparer who fails all three on one return owes $1,680 ($560 × 3). Across 40 returns, total exposure reaches $67,200. The per-credit structure creates meaningful deterrence: a per-return penalty would allow preparers to claim multiple unverified credits for only a single penalty, undermining the due diligence purpose. These credits are frequent targets of fraud — particularly EIC and AOTC — and the cumulative penalty structure reflects the heightened compliance risk. Choice A is incorrect because the penalty is per credit, not per return. Choice C is incorrect because there is no season-level cap. Choice D is incorrect because all four covered items trigger independent penalties.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1171;

-- ID 1174: Legal Duties and Responsibilities — Tax shelter position heightened standard analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA takes a position on a tax shelter transaction on a client''s return. A colleague takes an identical position on a non-shelter transaction for a different client. Both positions have approximately 45% likelihood of being sustained on the merits. Analyze how the standard for tax shelter positions under Section 6694(a) differs from the standard for non-shelter positions, and explain why Congress imposed a more demanding threshold for shelters.',
  choices = '["Both positions meet the applicable standard because ''substantial authority'' (approximately 40%) applies uniformly to all return positions", "The non-shelter position meets the ''substantial authority'' standard at 45% likelihood, but the tax shelter position fails because it must meet the ''more likely than not'' standard (greater than 50% likelihood) — Congress imposed the higher standard for tax shelters because these transactions are designed primarily for tax avoidance, carry heightened audit risk, and historically were marketed with aggressive legal opinions that overstated the likelihood of success; requiring the preparer to believe the position will ''more likely than not'' be sustained ensures a genuine analytical basis rather than reliance on manufactured authority", "Tax shelter positions have a lower standard (''reasonable basis'') because shelters involve complex legal issues that inherently have lower certainty", "Both positions automatically fail because any position with less than 75% likelihood triggers the Section 6694(a) penalty"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 6694(a)(2)(C) imposes the "more likely than not" standard (greater than 50% likelihood of being sustained on the merits) for tax shelter positions, compared to the "substantial authority" standard (approximately 40%) for non-shelter positions. At 45% likelihood, the non-shelter position clears its threshold while the identical shelter position falls short. Congress enacted the higher standard in response to the widespread marketing of abusive tax shelters in the 1990s and 2000s, where preparers relied on strained legal theories to support positions that often collapsed under IRS scrutiny. The "more likely than not" standard requires the preparer to have genuine confidence that the position would prevail if challenged — not merely that some technical argument could be constructed. This heightened scrutiny reflects the reality that tax shelter transactions disproportionately erode the tax base and that preparers serve as gatekeepers. Choice A is incorrect because the standards differ based on whether the transaction is a tax shelter. Choice C reverses the relationship — shelter positions face higher standards. Choice D is incorrect because no 75% threshold exists in the penalty provisions.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1174;

