-- ID 1287: Individual Taxation: Deductions — Domestic manufacturing auto loan deduction analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Two taxpayers each purchase a $45,000 vehicle in 2026 and finance the full amount. Harper buys a vehicle assembled in the United States by a domestic manufacturer, while Jensen buys an identical-class vehicle imported from overseas. Both pay $3,800 in auto loan interest during the year. Under H.R. 1, analyze how the domestic manufacturing requirement creates different tax outcomes for these otherwise identical vehicle purchases.',
  choices = '["Both taxpayers deduct the $3,800 in auto loan interest because the deduction applies to all vehicle purchases regardless of manufacturing origin", "Harper may deduct the $3,800 auto loan interest as an above-the-line deduction because the vehicle meets the U.S.-manufactured requirement, while Jensen receives no deduction for the auto loan interest because the imported vehicle does not qualify — the domestic manufacturing requirement is a targeted incentive designed to shift consumer purchasing toward domestically produced vehicles by creating a meaningful tax differential; for a taxpayer in the 24% bracket, Harper saves approximately $912 in federal tax that Jensen does not, solely because of where the vehicle was manufactured", "Jensen deducts the interest while Harper does not, because the deduction is designed to encourage international trade", "Both taxpayers deduct the interest, but Harper receives a double deduction as an additional incentive for buying domestic"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The auto loan interest deduction under H.R. 1 is conditioned on the vehicle being manufactured in the United States. Harper''s qualifying purchase allows the $3,800 interest deduction as an above-the-line adjustment to income, reducing AGI and taxable income. Jensen''s imported vehicle fails the domestic manufacturing test, making the auto loan interest nondeductible personal interest under Section 163(h). The tax differential is the entire deduction amount — at a 24% marginal rate, Harper saves approximately $912 that Jensen cannot claim. Additionally, as an above-the-line deduction, Harper''s AGI is reduced, potentially preserving eligibility for other AGI-sensitive benefits. This structure uses the tax code as an industrial policy tool, creating financial incentives for domestic purchasing without direct subsidies. Choice A is incorrect because the manufacturing origin requirement is a core eligibility condition. Choice C reverses the incentive direction. Choice D is incorrect because there is no double deduction mechanism — the deduction applies once to qualifying vehicles.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1287;

-- ID 2652: Individual Taxation: Deductions — Above-the-line educator expense classification analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Rivera, an eligible educator, pays $350 in unreimbursed classroom expenses (books and supplies) in 2026. The above-the-line deduction limit for educator expenses is $300. Rivera could alternatively claim the full $350 as an itemized deduction for unreimbursed employee business expenses (if available). Analyze why the above-the-line classification of the educator expense deduction benefits Rivera differently than an itemized deduction would, even when the itemized amount is larger.',
  choices = '["The itemized deduction is always more beneficial because it allows the full $350 deduction versus the $300 above-the-line cap", "The above-the-line classification is strategically more valuable because it reduces AGI, which is the measuring stick for numerous tax benefits — a lower AGI can preserve eligibility for education credits, expand the rental loss allowance, reduce Social Security benefit taxability, and lower net investment income tax exposure; additionally, the above-the-line deduction is available even to taxpayers who claim the standard deduction, while an itemized deduction requires foregoing the standard deduction — for most educators, the standard deduction exceeds total itemized deductions, making an itemized-only educator expense deduction worthless in practice", "Both deductions affect taxable income identically so there is no strategic difference between above-the-line and below-the-line classification", "The above-the-line deduction is less valuable because it phases out at lower income levels than itemized deductions"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The above-the-line vs. below-the-line distinction creates two critical differences. First, above-the-line deductions reduce AGI, which cascades through the return — a lower AGI can increase education credits, expand the $25,000 rental loss allowance, reduce the taxable percentage of Social Security benefits, and affect dozens of other AGI-sensitive provisions. Itemized deductions reduce taxable income but not AGI, providing no cascade benefit. Second, and more practically significant for most educators, the above-the-line deduction is available regardless of whether the taxpayer itemizes or takes the standard deduction. Since the 2017 TCJA nearly doubled the standard deduction, approximately 90% of filers take the standard deduction — meaning an itemized-only educator deduction would provide zero benefit to most teachers. The $300 above-the-line deduction, while capped, is accessible to all qualifying educators. The remaining $50 above the cap may be lost, but the $300 that is deductible provides more real-world benefit than a $350 itemized deduction that most educators cannot practically claim. Choice A ignores the standard deduction interaction. Choice C is incorrect because the AGI impact and standard deduction interaction create meaningful differences. Choice D is incorrect because the educator expense deduction has no phase-out.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 2652;

