-- ID 535: Like-Kind Exchanges — Related-party loss disallowance and subsequent sale interaction
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Franklin sells equipment with a basis of $80,000 to his controlled corporation for $55,000 (FMV), producing a $25,000 loss that Section 267 disallows. The corporation later sells the equipment to an unrelated buyer for $90,000. Analyze how the Section 267 loss disallowance interacts with the corporation''s subsequent sale — specifically, what is the net tax effect across both transactions.',
  choices = '["The corporation recognizes a $35,000 gain ($90,000 − $55,000 basis) and Franklin''s $25,000 disallowed loss is permanently forfeited — the combined tax effect is $35,000 of net recognized gain", "The corporation recognizes a $10,000 gain: its $35,000 gain ($90,000 − $55,000) is reduced by Franklin''s $25,000 previously disallowed loss under Section 267(d) — the disallowed loss is not permanently lost but instead offsets the related buyer''s subsequent gain, limiting the net recognized gain across both transactions to $10,000, which equals the true economic gain ($90,000 final sale − $80,000 original basis)", "The corporation recognizes $0 gain because related-party transactions are fully exempt from taxation", "Franklin can deduct the $25,000 loss on his personal return in the year the corporation sells to the third party"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 267(a)(1) disallows the $25,000 loss on the sale between related parties, but Section 267(d) provides partial relief on the subsequent sale. When the corporation sells to an unrelated party for $90,000, its realized gain is $35,000 ($90,000 − $55,000 basis). However, the corporation may offset this gain by up to $25,000 — the amount of Franklin''s previously disallowed loss. The net recognized gain is $10,000 ($35,000 − $25,000). This $10,000 equals the true economic gain across both transactions ($90,000 final price − $80,000 original basis), which is the economically correct result. The Section 267(d) mechanism ensures that the disallowed loss is not permanently forfeited but merely shifted in timing and taxpayer — it reduces the related buyer''s gain rather than providing a deduction to the original seller. Choice A is incorrect because Section 267(d) prevents permanent forfeiture by allowing the loss to offset the buyer''s subsequent gain. Choice C is incorrect because related-party transactions are not exempt from taxation. Choice D is incorrect because the loss benefit transfers to the corporation, not back to Franklin personally.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 535;

-- ID 537: Like-Kind Exchanges — Publicly traded securities installment method exclusion analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A taxpayer sells two assets at a gain: (1) publicly traded stock for $500,000 (basis $300,000) with payment in three annual installments, and (2) a rental property for $500,000 (basis $300,000) with the same three-year installment terms. Analyze why the installment method under Section 453 is available for the rental property sale but not the stock sale, and what policy rationale justifies excluding publicly traded securities.',
  choices = '["Both sales qualify for installment method treatment because Section 453 applies to any sale with deferred payments regardless of the asset type", "The stock sale is excluded from installment method treatment because publicly traded securities have a readily ascertainable market value — the seller can determine the full amount of gain at the time of sale and could convert the installment note to cash by selling it on the open market; allowing installment deferral would create an artificial timing benefit for taxpayers who voluntarily chose seller financing when they could have received full cash proceeds; the rental property qualifies because real estate lacks a liquid public market, making installment sales a genuine commercial necessity rather than a tax timing strategy", "The stock sale qualifies for installment method but the rental property does not, because real estate transactions are excluded from all deferral provisions", "Neither sale qualifies because the installment method was repealed for all asset types by the TCJA"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 453(k)(2) excludes sales of publicly traded securities (stocks, bonds, and other marketable securities) from installment method treatment. The rationale is that these securities have a readily ascertainable fair market value and trade on liquid markets. A taxpayer selling stock on an installment basis could just as easily sell the stock for immediate cash — choosing installment terms is a voluntary decision that should not generate tax deferral. The installment method was designed for situations where the seller genuinely cannot receive full payment at closing (such as real estate transactions where buyer financing is customary). Rental property lacks a public trading market, buyers often require seller financing, and the sale price may not be convertible to immediate cash — making installment deferral a necessary accommodation for legitimate commercial arrangements. Choice A is incorrect because Section 453(k) explicitly excludes certain asset types. Choice C reverses the treatment — real estate is one of the most common installment method transactions. Choice D is incorrect because the installment method remains available for qualifying sales.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 537;

-- ============================================================
-- PROFESSIONAL ETHICS IN TAX (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 2406: Professional Ethics in Tax — Competing obligations under SSTS No. 6
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A client informs their CPA, Walsh, that they failed to report $20,000 of cash income on a return filed two years ago. Walsh faces competing obligations: duty to the client (confidentiality and advocacy), duty to the tax system (promoting compliance), and the prohibition against assisting in fraud. Analyze why SSTS No. 6 requires Walsh to recommend amended filing rather than allowing Walsh to ignore the error, report directly to the IRS, or withdraw silently.',
  choices = '["SSTS No. 6 requires Walsh to recommend that the client file an amended return or otherwise correct the error, because this approach balances all competing obligations — it respects client confidentiality (Walsh does not report to the IRS without consent), fulfills the duty to the tax system (the client is advised of the legal obligation to correct), and avoids complicity in ongoing noncompliance; if the client refuses, Walsh should consider whether to continue the engagement but cannot unilaterally disclose to the IRS", "Walsh must immediately report the unreported income to the IRS because the CPA''s duty to the tax system overrides client confidentiality in all error situations", "Walsh should ignore the error because it occurred on a prior-year return and SSTS No. 6 applies only to current-year returns", "Walsh should silently withdraw from the engagement without informing the client, because withdrawal eliminates any ethical obligation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): SSTS No. 6 (Knowledge of Error: Return Preparation and Administrative Proceedings) requires the CPA to promptly inform the client of the error and recommend corrective action, which typically means filing an amended return. This approach threads the needle between competing obligations. The CPA cannot report to the IRS without client authorization — doing so would violate confidentiality under Section 7216 and state ethics rules. But the CPA also cannot pretend the error does not exist — ignoring known errors on future returns could constitute aiding and abetting understatement. Recommending correction fulfills the advisory role while leaving the ultimate decision with the client. If the client refuses to correct the error, Walsh must evaluate whether the client''s noncompliance affects the ability to continue the engagement, potentially requiring withdrawal. Choice B is incorrect because CPAs generally cannot disclose client information to the IRS without consent. Choice C is incorrect because SSTS No. 6 applies to errors discovered on any return. Choice D is incorrect because silent withdrawal without informing the client of the error does not satisfy the notification requirement.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 2406;

-- ID 2408: Professional Ethics in Tax — Prior-year error impact on current-year return
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is preparing a current-year return for a client who refused to file an amended return for a prior-year error that understated income by $30,000. The prior-year error does not directly affect any current-year line items — the understated income was a one-time event with no carryforward implications. Analyze how the prior-year error affects the CPA''s obligations when preparing the current-year return, and what the CPA should do if the prior-year error did have carryforward effects.',
  choices = '["The CPA may prepare the current-year return because the prior-year error does not affect current-year amounts — however, the CPA should document the client''s refusal to amend and consider whether the ongoing relationship is appropriate; if the prior-year error had carryforward effects (such as an incorrect NOL or basis), the CPA could NOT sign the current-year return without correcting those carried-forward amounts, because doing so would knowingly perpetuate an error on a return the CPA is signing", "The CPA must refuse to prepare any future returns for the client once any prior-year error is discovered, regardless of whether it affects the current year", "The CPA may prepare the current-year return and carry forward the incorrect amounts because the error originated in a prior year that the CPA did not prepare", "The CPA must report the client to the IRS before preparing any current-year return"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): When a prior-year error has no effect on current-year amounts, the CPA may continue to prepare the current-year return — there is nothing incorrect on the current return itself. However, the CPA should document the client''s refusal and evaluate whether the relationship should continue. The analysis changes significantly when the prior-year error has carryforward effects. If the $30,000 understatement affected basis, NOL, or credit carryforwards, those incorrect amounts would flow into the current year. The CPA cannot sign a current-year return containing amounts the CPA knows to be wrong. In that case, the CPA must use the correct figures on the current return, effectively correcting the carryforward impact even though the prior return remains unamended. This distinction — standalone error versus cascading error — is the key analytical framework for handling prior-year errors. Choice B is incorrect because the CPA is not required to terminate the relationship solely because a prior-year error exists. Choice C is incorrect because a CPA cannot knowingly carry forward incorrect amounts. Choice D is incorrect because CPAs generally cannot report clients to the IRS without consent.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 2408;

