-- ID 567: Estate and Gift Tax — Portability election and surviving spouse estate planning
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Harrison died in 2025 with a gross estate of $4,200,000, leaving the entire estate to his surviving spouse, Whitfield, via the unlimited marital deduction. The 2025 applicable exclusion amount is $13,610,000. Harrison''s executor timely files Form 706 and elects portability. Analyze how the portability election affects Whitfield''s future estate tax exposure, and what would happen if the executor had failed to make the election.',
  choices = '["Portability allows Whitfield to add Harrison''s unused exclusion ($13,610,000 − $0 taxable estate = $13,610,000 DSUE amount) to her own $13,610,000 exclusion, giving her a combined exclusion of approximately $27,220,000 — without the election, Whitfield''s exclusion would remain at $13,610,000, and any estate above that amount at her death would be subject to the 40% estate tax; the marital deduction eliminated Harrison''s estate tax but ''wasted'' his exclusion unless portability preserves it for Whitfield", "Portability has no effect because the marital deduction already eliminated all estate tax, making the exclusion amount irrelevant", "Portability transfers Harrison''s assets to Whitfield tax-free, which is the same function as the marital deduction", "The executor''s failure to elect portability would have no consequence because the surviving spouse automatically inherits the deceased spouse''s exclusion"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The unlimited marital deduction under Section 2056 eliminated Harrison''s estate tax — but this is a deferral, not a permanent benefit, because the assets will be included in Whitfield''s estate. Without portability, Harrison''s entire $13,610,000 exclusion would be wasted because his taxable estate is $0 after the marital deduction. The portability election under Section 2010(c)(4) preserves the Deceased Spousal Unused Exclusion (DSUE) amount — the difference between Harrison''s applicable exclusion and his taxable estate. Since the taxable estate is $0, the full $13,610,000 transfers to Whitfield. Combined with her own exclusion, Whitfield can shelter approximately $27,220,000 from estate tax. If the executor had not elected portability (by filing Form 706 within 9 months plus extensions), the DSUE would be permanently lost. This makes the portability election one of the most critical estate planning decisions, even when no estate tax is owed at the first death. Choice B is incorrect because the marital deduction only defers tax — the exclusion matters at the surviving spouse''s death. Choice C confuses portability with the marital deduction. Choice D is incorrect because portability requires an affirmative election via timely Form 706 filing.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 567;

-- ID 569: Estate and Gift Tax — Terminable interest rule and trust disqualification analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Under the unlimited marital deduction, most transfers between spouses are fully deductible. However, the terminable interest rule disqualifies certain transfers. Grayson creates a trust for his wife, Lennox, that provides income for her lifetime, but upon her death or remarriage, the remaining trust assets pass to Grayson''s children from a prior marriage. Analyze why the terminable interest rule disqualifies this trust from the marital deduction and what specific trust feature causes the disqualification.',
  choices = '["The trust is disqualified solely because it was created during Grayson''s lifetime rather than at death — the marital deduction applies only to testamentary transfers", "The trust is disqualified because it is a terminable interest — Lennox''s interest ends at her death or remarriage, and the remaining property passes to a third party (Grayson''s children) without being included in Lennox''s estate; the terminable interest rule exists because the marital deduction''s purpose is to defer taxation until the surviving spouse''s death, and if the property bypasses the surviving spouse''s estate entirely, the deferral becomes permanent avoidance — no estate tax would ever be collected on those assets at either spouse''s death", "The trust is disqualified because trust income distributions to a spouse never qualify for the marital deduction regardless of the trust terms", "The trust is disqualified solely because Grayson has children from a prior marriage, which automatically invalidates any marital deduction for the current spouse"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The terminable interest rule under Section 2056(b) disqualifies transfers where (1) the surviving spouse''s interest terminates upon the occurrence of an event or passage of time, and (2) another person receives the property interest after termination. Lennox''s trust interest terminates at death or remarriage, and the remainder passes to Grayson''s children — satisfying both conditions. The policy rationale is critical: the marital deduction is a deferral mechanism, not an exclusion. It assumes the transferred property will be included in the surviving spouse''s estate and taxed at that point. A terminable interest bypasses the surviving spouse''s estate entirely — the property goes to the children without ever being subject to estate tax in either estate. Congress enacted the terminable interest rule to prevent this permanent avoidance. Note that a Qualified Terminable Interest Property (QTIP) trust election under Section 2056(b)(7) can override this rule by requiring the trust property to be included in the surviving spouse''s estate. Choice A is incorrect because the marital deduction applies to both lifetime and testamentary transfers. Choice C is incorrect because trust income distributions can qualify with proper structuring. Choice D is incorrect because the existence of children from a prior marriage is irrelevant to the marital deduction analysis.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 569;

