-- ID 1182: Debtor-Creditor Relationships — PMSI 20-day grace period analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'On March 1, a seller delivers equipment to Baxter Corp. under a credit sale and retains a purchase money security interest (PMSI). The seller files a financing statement on March 18 — seventeen days after delivery. A competing creditor, First National Bank, had a previously perfected security interest in all of Baxter''s equipment, filed on January 15. Analyze why the 20-day grace period exists for PMSI perfection and what would happen to the seller''s priority if the filing had occurred on March 25 instead.',
  choices = '["The seller has priority over First National Bank because the PMSI filing on March 18 falls within the 20-day grace period under UCC Section 9-324(a), giving the seller retroactive priority from the date of delivery; the grace period exists because purchase money lenders enable the debtor to acquire new assets and should not be penalized for the practical delay between delivery and filing — without this protection, sellers would refuse credit sales unless filing occurred simultaneously with delivery, which is commercially impractical; if filing occurred on March 25 (day 24), the seller would lose PMSI super-priority and be subordinate to First National''s earlier-filed interest under the first-to-file rule", "The seller loses priority regardless of timing because previously filed security interests always defeat later interests without exception", "The 20-day grace period is 30 days, so both March 18 and March 25 filings would preserve priority", "The seller has priority only if the financing statement was filed before delivery, not after — the 20-day period runs before the delivery date"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): UCC Section 9-324(a) grants a PMSI in goods (other than inventory) super-priority over conflicting security interests if the PMSI is perfected within 20 days of the debtor receiving possession. The seller''s March 18 filing (day 17) is within the window, so the PMSI has priority over First National''s earlier-filed blanket lien — with retroactive effect to the delivery date. The policy rationale is commercial practicality: purchase money transactions enable debtors to acquire new productive assets, and the seller or lender who finances that acquisition deserves priority because their credit directly created the new collateral. Without the grace period, every credit sale would require simultaneous filing, adding cost and delay that would discourage equipment financing. If the seller filed on March 25 (day 24), the grace period would expire, the PMSI would be perfected but without super-priority, and First National''s earlier filing date would give it priority under the default first-to-file-or-perfect rule. Choice B is incorrect because the PMSI super-priority is a specific exception to the general rule. Choice C incorrectly states the grace period as 30 days. Choice D is incorrect because the 20-day period runs after delivery, not before.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 1182;

-- ID 1185: Debtor-Creditor Relationships — Buyer in ordinary course policy analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A buyer purchases inventory from a retailer in the ordinary course of business. The retailer had granted a bank a perfected security interest in all of its inventory. The buyer was unaware of the security interest. Under UCC Section 9-320(a), the buyer takes the inventory free of the bank''s security interest. Analyze the policy rationale behind this buyer-in-ordinary-course (BIOC) rule, and explain whether the outcome would change if the buyer had actual knowledge of the bank''s security interest at the time of purchase.',
  choices = '["Knowledge of the security interest would defeat the BIOC protection because buyers cannot take free of liens they know about — the rule only protects innocent purchasers", "The BIOC rule protects the buyer regardless of whether the buyer knew of the security interest — under Section 9-320(a), a buyer in the ordinary course takes free of a security interest created by the seller even if the buyer knows the interest exists; the rule exists because requiring every retail or wholesale purchaser to search UCC financing statement records before buying inventory would make commerce impractical; lenders who take security interests in inventory accept the risk that the debtor will sell it, and the lender''s remedy is the debtor''s obligation to account for proceeds, not a claim against the buyer", "The BIOC rule applies only to consumer purchases under $5,000 and would not protect a commercial buyer", "The bank''s security interest transfers automatically to the buyer along with the inventory, giving the bank a claim against the buyer''s assets"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The BIOC rule under UCC Section 9-320(a) provides that a buyer in the ordinary course of business takes free of a security interest created by the seller, even if the security interest is perfected and even if the buyer knows of its existence. The only exception is if the buyer knows the sale violates the terms of the security agreement — mere knowledge of the security interest''s existence is not enough. The policy rationale is commercial fluidity: inventory exists to be sold. A lender who takes a security interest in a retailer''s inventory implicitly expects the retailer to sell that inventory to customers. Requiring every buyer to conduct a UCC search before purchasing goods would paralyze commerce. Instead, the lender''s protection comes from the debtor''s obligation to account for sale proceeds and from the lender''s ability to claim a security interest in those proceeds under Section 9-315. Choice A is incorrect because knowledge of the security interest does not defeat BIOC protection — only knowledge that the sale violates the security agreement does. Choice C is incorrect because the BIOC rule applies to all buyers in the ordinary course, including commercial buyers. Choice D is incorrect because the security interest does not follow the goods into the buyer''s hands under the BIOC rule.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1185;

