-- AGENCY AND REGULATION (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 67: Agency and Regulation — Respondeat superior scope of employment analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornton Logistics hires driver Mitchell as an employee to deliver goods. During a delivery, Mitchell negligently causes an accident while taking a 15-minute detour to buy coffee. A customer, Parrish, is injured. In a separate incident, Mitchell causes an accident while using the company truck to run a personal errand 30 miles from the delivery route on a day off. Analyze how the scope of employment determination affects liability allocation between Thornton and Mitchell in each scenario.',
  choices = '["Thornton is liable in both scenarios because employers are always liable for any harm caused by employees regardless of the circumstances", "Under respondeat superior, Thornton is likely liable for the delivery-route detour because a minor deviation (brief coffee stop during a work trip) is generally considered within the scope of employment — Mitchell was still furthering the employer''s business purpose; Thornton is likely NOT liable for the personal errand because a 30-mile trip on a day off is a ''frolic'' that falls outside the scope of employment — the key analytical distinction is whether the employee''s conduct was a minor ''detour'' (still within scope) or a substantial ''frolic'' (outside scope); Mitchell is personally liable in both scenarios because agents are always liable for their own torts", "Mitchell is liable in both scenarios but Thornton has no liability in either because employers are never vicariously liable for employee negligence", "Thornton is liable only for the personal errand because the delivery-route accident was Mitchell''s fault alone"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Respondeat superior imposes vicarious liability on employers for employee torts committed within the scope of employment. The critical analytical framework distinguishes "detours" (minor deviations that do not take the employee outside the scope of employment) from "frolics" (substantial departures that sever the employment connection). Mitchell''s 15-minute coffee stop during a delivery is a minor detour — courts consistently hold that brief personal errands during work duties remain within scope because the employee is still generally furthering the employer''s business. The 30-mile personal trip on a day off is a frolic — Mitchell has completely abandoned the employer''s purpose and is pursuing purely personal objectives. Thornton bears no vicarious liability for the frolic. Importantly, Mitchell is personally liable for negligence in both scenarios — respondeat superior creates additional employer liability but does not relieve the employee of personal responsibility. Choice A is incorrect because employers are not liable for conduct outside the scope of employment. Choice C is incorrect because respondeat superior specifically creates employer vicarious liability. Choice D reverses the liability analysis for both scenarios.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 67;

-- ID 501: Agency and Regulation — Rule 10b-5 vs Section 11 element comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'An investor purchases stock in a public offering and later discovers the registration statement contained material misstatements. The investor also holds stock in a separate company where a corporate officer traded on material nonpublic information, depressing the stock price. Analyze the difference in elements the investor must prove under Section 11 of the Securities Act of 1933 (for the registration statement claim) versus Rule 10b-5 under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (for the insider trading claim), and identify why scienter is the key differentiating element.',
  choices = '["The investor must prove scienter (intent to deceive) under both Section 11 and Rule 10b-5 because all securities fraud requires proving the defendant''s mental state", "Under Section 11, the investor need not prove scienter, reliance, or even that the defendant caused the loss — the investor must show only a material misstatement in the registration statement and resulting damages, because Congress designed Section 11 as a near-strict-liability provision to ensure registration statement accuracy; under Rule 10b-5, the investor must prove scienter (intent to deceive or recklessness), materiality, reliance, causation, and damages — scienter is the critical differentiator because it transforms the claim from a documentation accuracy standard into a fraud standard requiring proof of the defendant''s culpable mental state", "Section 11 requires more elements than Rule 10b-5 because registration statements receive greater legal protection", "Neither claim requires scienter because securities law uses strict liability for all investor claims"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 11 and Rule 10b-5 represent fundamentally different enforcement approaches. Section 11 covers misstatements in registration statements and imposes near-strict liability — the plaintiff needs only show a material misstatement or omission and damages. The issuer is strictly liable; other signatories (directors, underwriters, accountants) have limited affirmative defenses (due diligence). No scienter, reliance, or causation need be proven by the plaintiff. Rule 10b-5 covers all securities fraud and requires the full panoply of fraud elements: (1) material misstatement or omission, (2) scienter (intent to deceive, manipulate, or defraud, or recklessness), (3) connection to purchase or sale of securities, (4) reliance, (5) economic loss, and (6) loss causation. Scienter is the critical differentiator because it requires proving the defendant''s mental state — that the officer knowingly or recklessly deceived investors. This higher burden reflects the broader scope of Rule 10b-5, which applies to all securities transactions, not just registered offerings. Choice A is incorrect because Section 11 does not require scienter. Choice C reverses the relative burden. Choice D is incorrect because Rule 10b-5 specifically requires scienter.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 501;

-- ============================================================
