-- ID 68: Business Structures — LLC vs general partnership liability analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Parker and Quinn form a general partnership to operate a consulting firm. Separately, Rivera and Santos form an LLC for an identical consulting business. A client sues each entity for $500,000 due to negligent advice provided by Parker (in the partnership) and Rivera (in the LLC). The entity assets in each case total only $200,000. Analyze how the choice between LLC and general partnership affects the personal liability exposure of the non-negligent owner (Quinn versus Santos) in each scenario.',
  choices = '["Both Quinn and Santos face identical personal liability because all business structures expose owners to unlimited personal liability for entity debts", "Quinn faces no personal liability while Santos is personally liable for the full $300,000 shortfall, because partnerships provide limited liability while LLCs do not", "Quinn, as a general partner, is personally liable for the $300,000 shortfall ($500,000 claim minus $200,000 entity assets) because general partners have unlimited joint and several liability for all partnership obligations — Santos, as an LLC member, is generally shielded from personal liability beyond the LLC''s assets because the LLC provides a liability barrier similar to a corporation; Quinn could lose personal savings, home equity, and other assets, while Santos''s maximum exposure is the $200,000 already invested in the LLC", "Both entities provide limited liability, but Quinn is liable because the partnership has fewer than three partners while Santos is protected because the LLC has exactly two members"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The general partnership structure provides no liability shield — each general partner is jointly and severally liable for all partnership obligations, including those arising from another partner''s negligence within the scope of partnership business. Quinn is personally liable for the $300,000 shortfall despite not committing the negligence. The LLC structure creates a statutory liability barrier between members'' personal assets and entity obligations. Santos''s maximum loss is the capital invested in the LLC — personal assets are generally protected. This fundamental difference in liability exposure is the primary reason LLCs have largely replaced general partnerships for new business formation. The only exceptions to LLC protection are piercing the veil (commingling assets, fraud, undercapitalization) and personal tortfeasor liability (Rivera remains personally liable for her own negligent acts). Choice A is incorrect because LLCs specifically provide limited liability protection. Choice B reverses the analysis entirely. Choice D is incorrect because the number of members does not determine liability protection — the entity type does.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 68;

-- ID 506: Business Structures — Bankruptcy priority distribution analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Meridian Corp. files Chapter 7 bankruptcy. After liquidating all assets, $800,000 is available for distribution. The claims are: $100,000 in administrative expenses (trustee fees, attorney costs), $150,000 in employee wages earned within 180 days of filing (each under $15,150), $400,000 in unsecured trade creditor claims, and $200,000 in equity holder claims. Analyze how the priority scheme allocates the $800,000 and explain why administrative expenses receive highest priority.',
  choices = '["All four classes split the $800,000 proportionally (pro rata) because bankruptcy law treats all creditors equally", "Administrative expenses ($100,000) are paid first in full, then employee wages ($150,000) in full, then the remaining $550,000 goes to trade creditors ($400,000 in full) with $150,000 remaining for equity holders — administrative expenses receive top priority because without professionals to administer the bankruptcy (trustees, attorneys, accountants), the orderly liquidation could not occur; if no one were willing to serve because payment was uncertain, the bankruptcy system would collapse; employee wages rank next as a social policy to protect workers from losing earned compensation; equity holders receive distributions only after all creditor classes are satisfied", "Equity holders are paid first because shareholders invested capital and deserve priority return of their investment", "Employee wages receive first priority, then equity holders, then trade creditors, then administrative expenses"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 507 of the Bankruptcy Code establishes a strict priority hierarchy. Administrative expenses (Section 507(a)(2)) receive highest priority because the bankruptcy system depends on professionals willing to administer estates — if trustee fees and legal costs were subordinated, no qualified professional would accept appointment, and orderly liquidation would be impossible. Employee wages (Section 507(a)(4)) rank next, reflecting Congress''s social policy that workers who provided labor should not bear the full economic loss of their employer''s failure, subject to per-employee and time-period caps. General unsecured creditors follow, receiving pro rata distribution from remaining assets. Equity holders are last — they accepted the risk of ownership and receive distributions only after all creditors are paid in full. In this scenario, the $800,000 covers administrative expenses ($100,000), employee wages ($150,000), and trade creditors ($400,000), with $150,000 remaining for equity holders (75% of their $200,000 claim). Choice A is incorrect because bankruptcy uses strict priority, not pro rata distribution across all classes. Choice C is incorrect because equity is the lowest priority, not the highest. Choice D completely reverses the statutory priority order.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 506;

