-- ID 80: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT — AMT preference item impact analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Martinez and Chen, married filing jointly, have regular tax liability of $45,000 and tentative minimum tax (TMT) of $52,000. Their AMT is $7,000 ($52,000 − $45,000). The largest AMT adjustment is $35,000 of state and local tax (SALT) deductions added back to taxable income. Analyze how the SALT deduction addback increases the couple''s total tax liability, and identify what specific change to their tax profile would eliminate the AMT entirely.',
  choices = '["The SALT deduction has no effect on AMT because state taxes are not an AMT preference item", "The $35,000 SALT deduction addback is the primary driver of the $7,000 AMT — state and local taxes are deductible for regular tax but must be added back for AMT purposes, increasing AMTI and pushing TMT above regular tax; eliminating the AMT would require reducing AMTI below the point where TMT equals regular tax, which could be achieved by reducing SALT deductions (living in a lower-tax state), increasing regular tax liability (losing other deductions), or generating AMT credits from prior years; the AMT effectively creates a floor that prevents SALT deductions from reducing total federal tax below a certain level", "The AMT is $52,000 (the full TMT amount) regardless of regular tax liability", "The couple can avoid AMT by electing not to claim the SALT deduction, but this always produces a worse overall result"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The AMT calculation adds back certain deductions and preferences to regular taxable income, creating Alternative Minimum Taxable Income (AMTI). State and local tax deductions are one of the largest addback items — the $35,000 SALT deduction that reduced regular tax must be added back for AMT, increasing AMTI significantly. This pushes the TMT to $52,000, exceeding regular tax of $45,000 by $7,000. The AMT functions as a parallel tax system that limits the benefit of certain deductions. To eliminate the AMT, the couple needs TMT to fall at or below regular tax. This could happen if: (1) SALT deductions decrease (reducing the addback), (2) other adjustments decrease, or (3) regular tax increases (from higher income or fewer non-AMT deductions). The SALT cap introduced by TCJA at $10,000 has reduced AMT exposure for many taxpayers by already limiting the deduction for regular tax purposes. Choice A is incorrect because SALT is one of the most significant AMT adjustments. Choice C confuses TMT with AMT — AMT is the excess of TMT over regular tax. Choice D is incorrect because in some cases, strategically managing deductions can produce a better overall result.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 80;

-- ID 2703: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT — AMT exemption phaseout impact analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Porter, a single taxpayer, has Alternative Minimum Taxable Income (AMTI) of $650,000 in 2026. The AMT exemption for single filers is approximately $88,100, with a phaseout beginning at $609,350. The phaseout reduces the exemption by 25 cents for every dollar of AMTI above the threshold. Analyze how the phaseout affects Porter''s AMT exemption amount, and explain why the phaseout creates an effective marginal rate higher than the statutory 28% AMT rate over the phaseout range.',
  choices = '["The phaseout has no effect because Porter''s AMTI is below the phaseout completion point", "Porter''s AMTI exceeds the phaseout threshold by $40,650 ($650,000 − $609,350), reducing the $88,100 exemption by $10,163 (25% × $40,650) to $77,937; over the phaseout range, each additional dollar of AMTI reduces the exemption by $0.25, which means $0.25 more of income is exposed to the 28% AMT rate — this creates an additional effective tax of $0.07 per dollar (28% × $0.25), pushing the effective marginal AMT rate to approximately 35% (28% + 7%) during the phaseout; this hidden rate increase is often overlooked in tax planning", "The exemption is completely eliminated once AMTI exceeds $609,350 by any amount, leaving Porter with a $0 exemption", "The phaseout increases the exemption amount to reward higher-income taxpayers for being subject to AMT"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The AMT exemption phaseout reduces the exemption by $0.25 for every $1.00 of AMTI above the threshold. Porter''s $40,650 excess AMTI ($650,000 − $609,350) triggers a $10,163 reduction ($40,650 × 25%), leaving a $77,937 exemption. The critical analytical insight is the effective marginal rate effect: within the phaseout range, each additional dollar of AMTI both (1) is taxed at the 28% AMT rate and (2) reduces the exemption by $0.25, exposing an additional $0.25 to the 28% rate. This second effect adds 7% (28% × 25%) to the effective marginal rate, creating an approximately 35% effective rate during the phaseout range. This "hidden" rate increase catches many taxpayers off guard and creates planning opportunities — timing income to fall outside the phaseout range can produce meaningful tax savings. The phaseout complete elimination occurs at AMTI of approximately $961,750 for single filers ($609,350 + $88,100/0.25). Choice A is incorrect because Porter is within the phaseout range, not below it. Choice C is incorrect because the phaseout is gradual, not a cliff. Choice D reverses the effect — the phaseout reduces the exemption.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 2703;

-- ============================================================
-- TAX PROCEDURES (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 244: Tax Procedures — Accuracy-related penalty vs reasonable cause defense analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Two taxpayers each understate tax by $15,000 on their returns. Taxpayer Abbott relied on the advice of a competent CPA who reviewed all relevant facts before recommending the position. Taxpayer Bradford took the same position without consulting any advisor and did not research the issue. Analyze when the 20% accuracy-related penalty under Section 6662 applies versus when the reasonable cause defense prevents it, and why Abbott is more likely to avoid the penalty than Bradford.',
  choices = '["The 20% penalty ($3,000 for each taxpayer) applies automatically to any understatement regardless of circumstances — there is no reasonable cause defense available", "The 20% penalty applies to both negligence and substantial understatement of income tax, but the reasonable cause and good faith defense under Section 6664(c) can eliminate it — Abbott likely avoids the penalty because reliance on a qualified professional who reviewed all relevant facts demonstrates reasonable cause and good faith; Bradford likely faces the $3,000 penalty (20% × $15,000) because taking a position without professional advice or independent research does not establish reasonable cause; the distinction turns on whether the taxpayer acted with ordinary business care and prudence in attempting to comply with the law", "The penalty applies only to taxpayers who deliberately understate income — negligent errors are always exempt", "Abbott faces a higher penalty because using a CPA constitutes willful tax avoidance"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 6662 imposes a 20% penalty on the portion of tax attributable to negligence, disregard of rules, or substantial understatement of income tax. However, Section 6664(c) provides that the penalty does not apply if the taxpayer shows reasonable cause and acted in good faith. Reliance on professional advice is the strongest reasonable cause defense — but it requires that the advisor was qualified, the taxpayer provided complete and accurate information, and the advice was reasonable on its face. Abbott meets all three conditions: a competent CPA reviewed all relevant facts. Bradford has no similar defense — taking a position without research or professional guidance demonstrates a lack of ordinary business care. The 20% penalty on $15,000 is $3,000. The reasonable cause defense is fact-intensive: courts examine the totality of circumstances including the taxpayer''s education, sophistication, the complexity of the issue, and the effort made to comply. Choice A is incorrect because the reasonable cause defense is explicitly codified. Choice C is incorrect because negligence and substantial understatement both trigger the penalty. Choice D is incorrect because consulting a CPA demonstrates good faith compliance, not avoidance.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 244;

-- ID 2429: Tax Procedures — Substantial omission statute of limitations extension analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A taxpayer filed a return reporting $100,000 of gross income but omitted $30,000 of gross income from a side business. The general statute of limitations for IRS assessment is three years. Analyze how the 25% omission threshold under Section 6501(e) extends the statute of limitations, and determine whether the threshold would be met if the omission were $20,000 instead of $30,000.',
  choices = '["The three-year statute applies regardless of the omission amount because there is no provision for extending the limitations period based on income omission", "Any omission of any amount automatically extends the statute to six years because all errors receive the same treatment", "At $30,000, the omission exceeds 25% of reported gross income ($100,000 × 25% = $25,000), triggering the six-year statute of limitations under Section 6501(e) — the IRS has six years from the filing date to assess additional tax; at $20,000, the omission is only 20% of reported gross income, falling below the 25% threshold, so the standard three-year statute applies; this bright-line test creates a significant cliff effect where $5,000 of additional unreported income doubles the IRS''s assessment window from three to six years", "The 25% threshold reduces the statute to one year rather than extending it, because smaller omissions receive longer scrutiny"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Section 6501(e)(1)(A) extends the assessment statute of limitations from three years to six years when the taxpayer omits more than 25% of gross income stated on the return. With $100,000 reported and $30,000 omitted, the omission ratio is 30% ($30,000/$100,000), exceeding the 25% threshold and triggering the six-year period. If the omission were $20,000, the ratio would be 20% ($20,000/$100,000), below the threshold, and the standard three-year period would apply. The analytical insight is the cliff effect: a taxpayer who omits $24,999 has three years of exposure, while one who omits $25,001 has six years. This bright-line approach provides administrative certainty but creates disproportionate consequences near the threshold. The 25% test uses gross income reported on the return as the denominator — not total correct gross income — which means the threshold is calculated against the amount the taxpayer actually stated. Note that adequate disclosure of an omitted item on the return can prevent the six-year extension. Choice A is incorrect because Section 6501(e) specifically provides the extended period. Choice B is incorrect because the six-year extension requires exceeding the 25% threshold. Choice D reverses the effect entirely.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 2429;

