-- ID 5290: Government Regulation of Business — Disclosure vs merit regulation analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Under the Securities Act of 1933, a company must file a registration statement with the SEC before conducting a public offering. The SEC reviews the statement for completeness and compliance but does not evaluate whether the investment is good or bad. Analyze the distinction between the disclosure-based approach that the SEC follows versus a merit-based approach that some state regulators use, and explain why the federal system chose disclosure regulation.',
  choices = '["The SEC uses a merit-based approach, approving only securities it believes are good investments for the public — this protects unsophisticated investors from risky offerings", "The SEC uses a disclosure-based approach: it requires companies to provide complete, accurate information about the offering (financial statements, risk factors, use of proceeds, management background) but does not evaluate the merits of the investment itself — investors receive the information and make their own decisions; the disclosure approach was chosen because (1) the government is poorly positioned to evaluate investment quality, (2) different investors have different risk tolerances, and (3) merit review would create moral hazard by implying government endorsement of approved securities; some state blue sky laws use merit review, creating a regulatory contrast where the same offering might be permissible federally but blocked at the state level", "The SEC neither requires disclosure nor evaluates merit — the 1933 Act relies entirely on private litigation to police securities markets", "The disclosure and merit approaches are identical in practice because both result in the SEC approving all registered offerings"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The Securities Act of 1933 establishes a disclosure-based regulatory framework. The SEC''s role is to ensure that registration statements contain complete and accurate information — it does not approve or disapprove securities, evaluate investment quality, or guarantee that investors will profit. This "sunlight is the best disinfectant" philosophy, attributed to Justice Brandeis, assumes that informed investors can make their own decisions. The disclosure approach was chosen for several policy reasons: the government lacks the expertise to evaluate every offering''s investment merit, different investors have different risk appetites (a speculative offering may be appropriate for some), and SEC "approval" would create moral hazard — investors might interpret approval as endorsement and reduce their own due diligence. By contrast, some state blue sky laws use merit review, where the state regulator can block an offering deemed "unfair" even if fully disclosed. This creates potential conflicts where an offering is cleared federally but blocked in certain states. Choice A is incorrect because the SEC explicitly does not evaluate investment merit. Choice C is incorrect because the 1933 Act mandates comprehensive disclosure. Choice D is incorrect because the approaches are fundamentally different in both theory and practice.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5290;

-- ID 5292: Government Regulation of Business — Section 10(b) vs Section 16(b) remedy analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A corporate officer, Hendricks, purchased 5,000 shares of his company''s stock three weeks before the board announced a major acquisition that doubled the stock price. Hendricks had access to material nonpublic information about the acquisition. Six months later, Hendricks sold the shares at a substantial profit. Both Section 10(b)/Rule 10b-5 and Section 16(b) of the Securities Exchange Act could potentially apply. Analyze why Section 10(b)/Rule 10b-5 is the more appropriate remedy for the insider trading aspect, and how Section 16(b) operates differently.',
  choices = '["Section 16(b) is the appropriate remedy because it specifically targets insider trading based on material nonpublic information and requires proof of scienter", "Section 10(b)/Rule 10b-5 is appropriate for the insider trading claim because it targets deceptive conduct based on material nonpublic information and requires proof of scienter (intent to defraud) — it addresses the fraud of trading while possessing an informational advantage over the market; Section 16(b) operates entirely differently as a prophylactic ''short-swing profit'' rule that requires disgorgement of any profit from purchase-and-sale within six months by corporate insiders (officers, directors, 10%+ shareholders), regardless of whether any insider information was used — Section 16(b) requires no proof of scienter, fraud, or use of nonpublic information, making it a mechanical timing-based rule rather than a fraud-based remedy", "Neither provision applies because the officer held the shares for more than 30 days", "Both provisions are identical in their elements and remedies, differing only in which court has jurisdiction"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 10(b) and Rule 10b-5 address securities fraud — the deceptive practice of trading based on material nonpublic information. The plaintiff must prove scienter (intent to deceive or recklessness), materiality, reliance, causation, and damages. This fraud-based framework directly targets Hendricks''s informational advantage over other market participants. Section 16(b) is fundamentally different — it is a prophylactic rule that captures any profit from a purchase and sale (or sale and purchase) within a six-month window by statutory insiders (officers, directors, 10%+ shareholders). It operates mechanically: if the timing matches, the profit is disgorged to the corporation regardless of whether the insider used any nonpublic information. Hendricks''s six-month holding period means Section 16(b) may not even apply if the purchase-to-sale period exceeds six months. For the insider trading element, Rule 10b-5 is the appropriate vehicle because it specifically addresses the use of material nonpublic information. Choice A is incorrect because Section 16(b) does not require scienter or proof of insider information use. Choice C is incorrect because holding period is relevant to 16(b) but not 10b-5. Choice D is incorrect because the provisions differ fundamentally in elements, purpose, and remedy.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5292;

