-- ID 5311: Alternative Minimum Tax — Private activity bond interest AMT preference analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Whitaker holds $200,000 in private activity municipal bonds that generated $8,000 in tax-exempt interest during 2025. For regular tax purposes, the interest is excluded from gross income under Section 103. However, Whitaker''s tax advisor warns that the interest creates an AMT preference item. Analyze why private activity bond interest is treated as an AMT preference when other municipal bond interest is not, and how this affects a taxpayer who holds such bonds.',
  choices = '["All municipal bond interest is an AMT preference item — there is no distinction between private activity bonds and other municipal bonds under the AMT", "Private activity bond interest is an AMT preference because these bonds finance private commercial projects (stadiums, airports, housing developments) rather than core governmental functions — Congress determined that the tax-exempt subsidy is less justified for private beneficiaries and added the AMT preference to recapture some of the benefit from higher-income taxpayers; general obligation bonds financing core government services (schools, roads) are not AMT preferences because the public benefit justification is stronger; for Whitaker, the $8,000 is added back to taxable income when computing AMTI, potentially triggering or increasing AMT liability even though it remains tax-free for regular tax purposes", "Private activity bond interest reduces AMT liability rather than increasing it because the AMT rewards tax-exempt investing", "The AMT preference only applies to private activity bonds purchased before 2009 — all bonds purchased after that date are fully exempt from AMT"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The AMT distinction between private activity bonds and governmental bonds reflects a policy judgment about the strength of the tax exemption''s justification. Private activity bonds under Section 141 finance projects that primarily benefit private parties (corporate headquarters, sports facilities, industrial parks) even though they are issued by governmental entities. Congress viewed the tax-exempt interest subsidy as less warranted for these private beneficiaries and added the interest as an AMT preference item under Section 57(a)(5). General obligation bonds and essential function governmental bonds finance core public services — the tax exemption''s public benefit justification is stronger, so no AMT preference applies. For Whitaker, the $8,000 of private activity bond interest is added to taxable income when computing AMTI. If this adjustment, combined with others, pushes TMT above regular tax, Whitaker owes AMT — effectively paying tax on income that is "tax-exempt" for regular purposes. This dual treatment often surprises investors who assumed municipal bond interest was completely tax-free. Choice A is incorrect because only private activity bond interest is an AMT preference. Choice C reverses the effect. Choice D is incorrect because the AMT preference applies to currently issued private activity bonds as well.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 5311;

-- ID 5313: Alternative Minimum Tax — TMT and regular tax relationship analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Brennan computes regular tax liability of $45,000 and a tentative minimum tax (TMT) of $42,000 for 2025. Brennan''s colleague, Sato, computes regular tax of $45,000 and TMT of $51,000. Analyze the relationship between TMT and regular tax in each case, determine each taxpayer''s AMT liability, and identify what specific change to Brennan''s tax profile would trigger an AMT liability.',
  choices = '["Brennan owes $42,000 AMT and Sato owes $51,000 AMT because the AMT replaces regular tax in both cases", "Both owe $0 AMT because the TMT is always added to regular tax rather than compared against it", "Brennan owes $0 AMT because TMT ($42,000) does not exceed regular tax ($45,000) — the AMT applies only when TMT exceeds regular tax, and the excess is the AMT amount; Sato owes $6,000 AMT ($51,000 TMT − $45,000 regular tax); Brennan''s AMT would be triggered by any change that either increases TMT (such as claiming more AMT preference items or increasing SALT deductions) or decreases regular tax (such as losing income that doesn''t affect AMTI) — even a $3,001 swing would create AMT liability", "Brennan owes $3,000 AMT and Sato owes $6,000 AMT because AMT is always half the difference between TMT and regular tax"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): AMT equals the excess of tentative minimum tax over regular tax — if TMT does not exceed regular tax, AMT is $0. Brennan''s TMT ($42,000) is less than regular tax ($45,000), so AMT is $0. Brennan''s total tax is simply $45,000. Sato''s TMT ($51,000) exceeds regular tax ($45,000) by $6,000, creating a $6,000 AMT added to the $45,000 regular tax for a total of $51,000. The analytical insight is that Brennan is only $3,000 away from triggering AMT. Any change that increases TMT by more than $3,000 (adding AMT preference items like private activity bond interest, exercising incentive stock options, or increasing SALT deductions that are added back for AMT) or decreases regular tax by more than $3,000 (losing deductions that reduce regular tax but not AMTI) would push TMT above regular tax and create AMT liability. This near-miss position is common for taxpayers with moderate SALT deductions. Choice A confuses AMT with TMT — AMT is the excess amount, not the full TMT. Choice B incorrectly describes the AMT mechanism. Choice D incorrectly applies a 50% factor that does not exist in the AMT calculation.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 5313;

