-- ID 1156: Federal Tax Procedures — Statute of limitations commencement analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Three taxpayers file their 2024 returns under different circumstances: Andrews files timely on April 15, 2025; Barrett files early on February 10, 2025; and Crawford files late on August 20, 2025 (without an extension). Analyze how each filing scenario affects when the three-year statute of limitations for IRS assessment begins running, and explain the rule that creates different outcomes for early versus late filers.',
  choices = '["All three statutes begin on the actual filing date, so Andrews''s expires April 15, 2028; Barrett''s expires February 10, 2028; and Crawford''s expires August 20, 2028", "Andrews''s and Barrett''s statutes both begin on April 15, 2025 (the due date) and expire April 15, 2028, because returns filed before the due date are treated as filed on the due date under Section 6501(b)(1) — this prevents early filers from gaining a shorter effective assessment window by filing months before the deadline; Crawford''s statute begins August 20, 2025 (the actual filing date) and expires August 20, 2028, because late-filed returns start the clock when actually filed — there is no ''relating back'' to the due date for late filers, which means Crawford gave the IRS a longer total assessment window from the original due date", "The statute does not begin until the IRS processes the return, regardless of when it was filed", "Barrett''s early filing extends the statute to six years as a penalty for filing before the deadline"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 6501(b)(1) provides that returns filed before the due date are deemed filed on the due date. Barrett''s February 10 filing is treated as filed on April 15, 2025, so the three-year statute runs from April 15, 2025 to April 15, 2028 — identical to Andrews. This prevents early filers from gaining a shorter IRS assessment period; without this rule, filing on January 1 would give the IRS two months less assessment time than filing on April 15. Crawford filed after the due date, so the statute begins on the actual filing date (August 20, 2025) and runs three years to August 20, 2028. Note that from the perspective of the original due date, Crawford has effectively given the IRS approximately 3 years and 4 months of assessment time rather than exactly 3 years. Late filing provides no benefit to the taxpayer — it only extends the IRS''s effective assessment window. Choice A is incorrect because early-filed returns are treated as filed on the due date. Choice C is incorrect because the statute begins at filing, not processing. Choice D is incorrect because early filing has no penalty — it simply relates back to the due date.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1156;

-- ID 1158: Federal Tax Procedures — Civil fraud vs accuracy-related penalty comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The IRS examines two taxpayers who each understated tax by $50,000. For Taxpayer Adkins, the IRS determines the understatement resulted from negligence — Adkins failed to keep adequate records and made careless errors. For Taxpayer Boyd, the IRS discovers fabricated deductions and falsified documents, establishing intentional fraud. Analyze how the civil fraud penalty under Section 6663 compares to the accuracy-related penalty under Section 6662, and explain what evidentiary threshold determines which penalty applies.',
  choices = '["Both taxpayers face the same 20% penalty ($10,000) because all understatements are treated identically regardless of intent", "Adkins faces the 20% accuracy-related penalty under Section 6662 ($10,000), while Boyd faces the 75% civil fraud penalty under Section 6663 ($37,500) — the determining factor is the taxpayer''s mental state: Section 6662 covers negligence, disregard of rules, and substantial understatement, requiring no proof of intent; Section 6663 requires the IRS to prove fraud by clear and convincing evidence, a significantly higher evidentiary burden than the preponderance standard for Section 6662; the 75% rate reflects the severity of intentional tax evasion and the difficulty of detection; additionally, the fraud penalty has no statute of limitations — the IRS can assess it at any time, while Section 6662 is subject to the standard three-year limitations period", "Boyd faces a lower penalty than Adkins because the IRS offers reduced penalties to taxpayers who cooperate with fraud investigations", "Both penalties are criminal sanctions that can result in imprisonment"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The distinction between Section 6662 (accuracy-related, 20%) and Section 6663 (civil fraud, 75%) turns on the taxpayer''s intent. Section 6662 addresses non-intentional failures — negligence (failure to make reasonable attempts to comply), disregard of rules, and substantial understatements — and imposes a 20% penalty on the understatement. The IRS bears a preponderance-of-the-evidence burden. Section 6663 addresses intentional fraud — willful attempts to evade tax through falsified records, fabricated deductions, or concealment of income — and imposes a 75% penalty. The IRS must prove fraud by clear and convincing evidence, a higher standard reflecting the severity of the accusation. Boyd''s fabricated deductions and falsified documents constitute classic fraud indicators. The penalties are mutually exclusive for the same item — the IRS cannot impose both Section 6662 and Section 6663 on the same understatement. Critically, there is no statute of limitations for fraud assessments under Section 6501(c)(1), giving the IRS unlimited time to pursue fraudulent returns. Choice A is incorrect because the penalties differ substantially in rate and standard. Choice C is incorrect because fraud penalties are higher, not lower. Choice D is incorrect because these are civil penalties, not criminal sanctions — criminal tax evasion is prosecuted separately under Section 7201.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1158;

